{
  num: 2,
  file: 'Episode 2 - Geometric Sequences',
  segs: [
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Welcome back to Working It Out. I am Michael.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And I am Tyla, still asking the difficult questions.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode two. Geometric sequences.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `First, the question I left you with. Our sequence was two, nine, sixteen, and so on. Which term is equal to seven hundred and two?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `I used the rule from last time. Seven n minus five equals seven hundred and two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Good. Add five to both sides.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Seven n equals seven hundred and seven. So n is one hundred and one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The hundred and first term. And notice something important. Your answer for n came out as a whole number. That is your proof that seven hundred and two really is in the sequence. If n had come out as a fraction, the answer would be no, that number is not in the sequence.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now, today everything changes. Last time we added the same amount each time. Today we multiply.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Look at these. Two. Four. Eight. Sixteen. What is happening?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `It doubles every time. So the next one is thirty two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Correct. And when you multiply by the same number every time, that is called a geometric sequence.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So arithmetic adds, and geometric multiplies.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is the whole difference. Hold on to it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is today's real question. Same paper as last time. November two thousand and fourteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The first three terms of a geometric sequence are sixteen, four, one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Calculate the twelfth term. And leave your answer in simplified exponential form.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Wait. These numbers are getting smaller, not bigger.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `They are. And that tells you something straight away. Keep that in mind.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Okay. To find what we multiply by, I divide. Sixteen divided by four is four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test it. Start at sixteen and multiply by four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Sixteen times four is sixty four. But the second term is four, not sixty four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So your four is upside down. You divided the earlier term by the later term. It must be the other way around.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Later divided by earlier. Four divided by sixteen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is one quarter.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One quarter. And that is why the numbers are shrinking. When you multiply by a fraction smaller than one, the sequence gets smaller every step.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That number has an exam name. It is called the common ratio, and we write it as r.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Ratio means you find it by dividing. Common means every step shares it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I check it twice, like last time?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Always. Second term divided by first. Then third divided by second.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One divided by four is also one quarter. Same answer.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Then it really is geometric, and we may use the formula.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Build it with me. We start at sixteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `To reach the second term, how many times do you multiply by one quarter?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One time.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the third term?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two times. … This is the same counting as last week. One less than the term number.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Exactly the same idea. So the rule is, the enth term equals the first term, times the common ratio, to the power of n minus one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So the power just counts the multiplications.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The power counts the steps. Arithmetic multiplies the steps by d. Geometric raises r to the number of steps.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Write down what you know.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The first term is sixteen. The common ratio is one quarter. And n is twelve.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Substitute.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The twelfth term equals sixteen, times one quarter, to the power of, eleven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now, the exam said simplified exponential form. That means one base with one power. So make everything a power of two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Sixteen is two to the power of four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And one quarter is one over two squared. So that is two to the power of negative two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So now put those in.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two to the power of four, times two to the power of negative two, to the power of eleven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `When a power is raised to another power, you multiply the powers.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative two times eleven is negative twenty two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And when you multiply powers of the same base, you add the powers.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Four plus negative twenty two is negative eighteen. So the answer is two to the power of negative eighteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three marks. And notice the answer is a tiny number, which fits, because the sequence is shrinking.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Four things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. The ratio is later term divided by earlier term. Get that the wrong way round and every answer after it is wrong.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. The power is n minus one, not n. Same counting as arithmetic.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. If the question says exponential form, do not give a decimal. They want one base and one power.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number four. If the terms change sign, plus, minus, plus, minus, then your ratio is negative. Keep the minus sign inside the bracket.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. A geometric sequence multiplies by the same amount each time. That amount is the common ratio.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. The rule is the first term, times r to the power of n minus one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three. A ratio smaller than one makes the sequence shrink. A ratio bigger than one makes it grow.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question. Same sequence. Sixteen, four, one. Which term is equal to one over one thousand and twenty four?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `I will give you the answer next time. And next time we meet a pattern that is neither of these two. It does not add the same amount. It does not multiply by the same amount.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Then how do you even find the rule?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You look at the differences. And then you look at the differences again. That is episode three. Quadratic number patterns.` },
  ],
  slides: [
