{
  num: 5,
  file: 'Episode 5 - Finite Arithmetic Series',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode five. Finite arithmetic series.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Last time we wrote the instruction to add. Today we actually do the adding.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two hundred and fifty one numbers.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two hundred and fifty one numbers. In one line, on your calculator, in about twenty seconds.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Prove it. But first, my homework.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Your series was five, plus eight, plus eleven, and so on, up to forty seven.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The first term is five and the common difference is three. So the rule is three n plus two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And how did you find where it stops?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `I made the rule equal to forty seven. Three n plus two equals forty seven. So three n is forty five, and n is fifteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So sigma, from n equals one, to fifteen, of three n plus two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And hold on to that fifteen. You are going to need it again in about four minutes.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Add up every whole number from one to ten. Do not use a calculator.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One plus two is three, plus three is six, plus four is ten…` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Stop. There is a faster way, and a schoolboy worked it out about two hundred years ago. Take the first number and the last number, and add them.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One plus ten. Eleven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the second and the second last.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two plus nine. Also eleven. And three plus eight is eleven too.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Every pair makes eleven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Every pair. And how many pairs are there, out of ten numbers?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Five. So five times eleven. Fifty five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is the whole idea behind the formula. Pair them up from the outside in.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Write what you just did in symbols. There were ten numbers, so five pairs. Five is ten divided by two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And each pair was the first term plus the last term.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the sum is n over two, times first plus last. The exam writes the sum as S with a little n.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I only need three things. How many terms, the first one, and the last one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Only three. And when the question does not give you the last term, there is a second version that builds it for you.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Look inside the bracket. There is our n minus one again, from episode one. It is the same idea wearing a different coat.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the real one. Same paper, same series, one last question.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Calculate the sum of the series. Two plus nine plus sixteen, and so on, two hundred and fifty one terms.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And we already know almost everything about this one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You know all of it. Tell me what you have.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The first term is two. There are two hundred and fifty one terms. And from episode one, the last term is one thousand seven hundred and fifty two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I add the first and the last. Two plus one thousand seven hundred and fifty two is one thousand seven hundred and fifty four. Then times two hundred and fifty one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test that on the small one. One to ten. First plus last is eleven, times ten numbers.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `A hundred and ten. But the real answer was fifty five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Exactly double. Why?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Because I counted every number twice. Each pair used two numbers, so there are only half as many pairs.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So you must halve it. That is why the formula says n over two, not n.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Do it properly. Write the formula first.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `S two hundred and fifty one equals two hundred and fifty one, over two, times, first plus last.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Substitute.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two hundred and fifty one over two, times, two plus one thousand seven hundred and fifty two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The bracket is one thousand seven hundred and fifty four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And two hundred and fifty one divided by two is a decimal. Do not panic. That is allowed.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `A hundred and twenty five comma five, times one thousand seven hundred and fifty four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two hundred and twenty thousand, one hundred and twenty seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now check it with the other version, the one that does not need the last term.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two hundred and fifty one over two, times, two times two, plus two hundred and fifty, times seven.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Four plus one thousand seven hundred and fifty is one thousand seven hundred and fifty four. Same bracket.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Same bracket, same answer. Two formulas, one truth. Use whichever one the question gives you the pieces for.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Four things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Divide by two. You are counting pairs, not numbers. Forgetting this doubles your answer.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. n is how many terms. It is never the value of the last term.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. If the question gives you the last term, use the first plus last version. It is much faster and there is less to get wrong.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number four. A decimal in the middle of your working is fine. Only the final answer needs to make sense.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. Pair from the outside in. First with last, second with second last.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. The sum is n over two, times first plus last.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three. If you do not know the last term, use n over two, times two a plus n minus one d.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question. Your homework series from last time. Five, plus eight, plus eleven, up to forty seven. You already found there are fifteen terms. Now add them all up.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `I will give you the answer next time. And next time we add up a geometric series instead. The one that multiplies.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Can you still pair those from the outside in?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You cannot. Which is why geometric gets its own formula, and its own episode. That is episode six.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n: 1, afterSeg: 0, title: `EPISODE 5 • FINITE ARITHMETIC SERIES`, onScreen: `EPISODE 5 — FINITE ARITHMETIC SERIES` },
    { n: 2, afterSeg: 1, title: `WROTE IT VERSUS CALCULATE IT`, onScreen: `last time we WROTE it · today we CALCULATE it` },
    { n: 3, afterSeg: 3, title: `251 NUMBERS IN ONE LINE`, onScreen: `251 numbers · one line · about 20 seconds` },
    { n: 4, afterSeg: 5, title: `LAST TIME • WRITE IT IN SIGMA`, onScreen: `5 + 8 + 11 + … + 47` },
    { n: 5, afterSeg: 6, title: `FIND THE RULE`, onScreen: `a = 5, d = 3 → Tₙ = 3n + 2` },
    { n: 6, afterSeg: 8, title: `HOW MANY TERMS`, onScreen: `3n + 2 = 47 → n = 15` },
    { n: 7, afterSeg: 9, title: `ANSWER • SIGMA NOTATION`, onScreen: `∑ from n = 1 to 15 of (3n + 2) ✓` },
    { n: 8, afterSeg: 11, title: `WARM UP • ADD 1 TO 10`, onScreen: `1 + 2 + 3 + … + 10` },
    { n: 9, afterSeg: 14, title: `FIRST PLUS LAST`, onScreen: `1 + 10 = 11` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg: 16, title: `EVERY PAIR MAKES 11`, onScreen: `2 + 9 = 11 · 3 + 8 = 11 · 4 + 7 = 11 · 5 + 6 = 11` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg: 19, title: `ANSWER • 55`, onScreen: `5 × 11 = 55 ✓` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg: 21, title: `THE NUMBER OF PAIRS`, onScreen: `number of pairs = n ÷ 2` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg: 22, title: `EACH PAIR IS FIRST PLUS LAST`, onScreen: `each pair = first + last` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg: 23, title: `THE FORMULA • FIRST PLUS LAST`, onScreen: `Sₙ = n/2 (a + l)` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg: 25, title: `THE FORMULA • WITHOUT THE LAST TERM`, onScreen: `Sₙ = n/2 [2a + (n − 1)d]` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg: 26, title: `N MINUS 1 AGAIN`, onScreen: `(n − 1) — the same step counting as Episode 1` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg: 27, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • NOVEMBER 2014`, onScreen: `NSC Mathematics P1 · November 2014 · Question 2.4` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg: 28, title: `THE QUESTION • THE SUM`, onScreen: `2.4 Calculate the sum of the series.` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg: 31, title: `WE ALREADY HAVE EVERYTHING`, onScreen: `a = 2 · n = 251 · last term = 1 752 (from Episode 1)` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg: 32, title: `NO HALVING`, onScreen: `Tyla's answer: 251 × 1 754` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg: 34, title: `TEST IT SMALL • DOUBLE`, onScreen: `red cross · 10 × 11 = 110, but the sum is 55` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg: 37, title: `COUNTING PAIRS`, onScreen: `divide by 2 — you are counting PAIRS` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg: 39, title: `WRITE THE FORMULA`, onScreen: `S₂₅₁ = 251/2 (a + l)` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg: 41, title: `SUBSTITUTE`, onScreen: `S₂₅₁ = 251/2 (2 + 1 752)` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg: 42, title: `THE BRACKET`, onScreen: `S₂₅₁ = 251/2 × 1 754` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg: 44, title: `THE DECIMAL IS FINE`, onScreen: `= 125,5 × 1 754` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg: 45, title: `ANSWER • 220 127`, onScreen: `S₂₅₁ = 220 127 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg: 47, title: `CHECK THE OTHER WAY`, onScreen: `S₂₅₁ = 251/2 [2(2) + 250(7)]` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg: 48, title: `SAME BRACKET`, onScreen: `[4 + 1 750] = 1 754 — the same` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg: 50, title: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS`, onScreen: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg: 51, title: `MARK LOSS 1 • ALWAYS HALVE`, onScreen: `1. n over two — always halve it` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg: 52, title: `MARK LOSS 2 • N COUNTS TERMS`, onScreen: `2. n counts terms` },
    { n: 33, afterSeg: 53, title: `MARK LOSS 3 • USE FIRST PLUS LAST`, onScreen: `3. last term given? use Sₙ = n/2 (a + l)` },
    { n: 34, afterSeg: 54, title: `MARK LOSS 4 • DECIMALS ARE FINE`, onScreen: `4. a decimal mid-working is normal` },
    { n: 35, afterSeg: 55, title: `REMEMBER`, onScreen: `REMEMBER` },
    { n: 36, afterSeg: 56, title: `RECAP 1 • PAIR OUTSIDE IN`, onScreen: `1. pair from the outside in` },
    { n: 37, afterSeg: 57, title: `RECAP 2 • FIRST PLUS LAST`, onScreen: `2. Sₙ = n/2 (a + l)` },
    { n: 38, afterSeg: 58, title: `RECAP 3 • THE OTHER VERSION`, onScreen: `3. Sₙ = n/2 [2a + (n − 1)d]` },
    { n: 39, afterSeg: 59, title: `YOUR TURN • FIND THE SUM`, onScreen: `5 + 8 + 11 + … + 47 · n = 15. Find the sum.` },
    { n: 40, afterSeg: 60, title: `PAUSE AND TRY`, onScreen: `PAUSE AND TRY` },
