{
  num: 6,
  file: 'Episode 6 - Finite Geometric Series',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode six. Finite geometric series.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Last time we added up a sequence that grows by adding. Today we add up one that grows by multiplying.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And you told me I cannot pair those from the outside in.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You cannot. So we need a different trick. And it is a beautiful one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `First, my homework.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Your series was five, plus eight, plus eleven, up to forty seven. Fifteen terms.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The first term is five. The last term is forty seven. And there are fifteen terms.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So which version did you use?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The first plus last one. Fifteen over two, times, five plus forty seven.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The bracket is fifty two. I halved the fifty two instead of the fifteen. That gave twenty six. Then fifteen times twenty six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three hundred and ninety.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three hundred and ninety. And halving the fifty two first was smart. You avoided the decimal completely.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now watch what happens if you take that same formula into today's topic. Here is a small geometric series. Two, plus four, plus eight.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `I can just add those. Fourteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You can. Now do it with last week's formula, and see what it gives you.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three over two, times, two plus eight. That is one comma five, times ten. Fifteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Fifteen. But the real answer is fourteen. So the arithmetic formula is wrong here. Why?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Because the pairs are not equal any more. Two and eight make ten, but the middle number is four, not five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Exactly. Pairing only works when the steps are equal. When the numbers multiply, the pairs go out of balance. So we need something new.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is the trick. Take the series, multiply the whole thing by the common ratio, and then subtract.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Subtract it from itself?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Watch. Take two, plus four, plus eight, plus sixteen. Call the total S.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now multiply every term by two, because two is the ratio.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Four, plus eight, plus sixteen, plus thirty two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now subtract the first line from the second.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The four cancels. The eight cancels. The sixteen cancels. Only thirty two and two are left.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So two S, minus S, is thirty two minus two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So S is thirty.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Check it by adding. Two, four, eight, sixteen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Thirty. It works.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now do exactly that with letters. First term a. Common ratio r. And n terms.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Multiply the whole line by r.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Subtract. Everything in the middle cancels again. You are left with a r to the n, minus a.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Take S out on the left, and take a out on the right.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So S is a, times r to the n minus one, all over r minus one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is on your formula sheet. And there is a second version, for when the ratio is a fraction, so the bottom stays positive.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Same formula, top and bottom both turned around. Use whichever one keeps you out of negatives.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the real one. Same paper, and the same sequence we used in episode two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Sixteen, four, one, and it carries on. Calculate the sum of the first ten terms.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The first term is sixteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the common ratio? Careful here.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `You divide, you do not subtract. Four divided by sixteen. That is a quarter.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Check it once more.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One divided by four is also a quarter. Same answer. So it really is geometric.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The ratio is less than one, so use the version with one minus r on the bottom.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Sixteen, times, one minus a quarter to the power of ten, all over, one minus a quarter.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Do the bottom first.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One minus a quarter is three quarters.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And sixteen divided by three quarters?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Sixteen times four over three. That is sixty four over three. Twenty one comma three three three, and it carries on.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the top. A quarter, to the power of ten.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is about one over a million. Almost nothing.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So the top bracket is almost exactly one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Which means the answer is almost exactly sixty four over three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Twenty one comma three three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two marks. And notice something. Ten terms, and the total has barely moved off twenty one comma three three. Hold that thought.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Four things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. You find r by dividing, never by subtracting. Later term, divided by earlier term.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. n counts terms. Ten terms means n is ten. It is never the value of a term.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. Do not round in the middle. Keep the fraction until the end, and round only your final answer.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number four. If the ratio is a fraction, use the one minus version. Both are on the formula sheet, and both give the same answer, but one keeps you out of negatives.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. Arithmetic adds the same amount. Geometric multiplies by the same amount. The series formula follows the sequence.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. The trick is to multiply by r and subtract. Everything in the middle cancels.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three. The sum is a, times r to the n minus one, over r minus one. Or one minus r to the n, over one minus r, when the ratio is a fraction.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question. The same series. Sixteen, plus four, plus one, and so on. This time add up twenty terms instead of ten.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `I will give you the answer next time. And I think the answer will surprise you.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Why would twenty terms surprise me?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Because of how little it changes. That is episode seven. We add up an endless number of terms, and still get a finite answer.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:  0, title: `EPISODE 6 • FINITE GEOMETRIC SERIES`, onScreen: `EPISODE 6 — FINITE GEOMETRIC SERIES` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:  1, title: `ADD VERSUS MULTIPLY`,                 onScreen: `last time it ADDED · today it MULTIPLIES` },
    { n:  3, afterSeg:  3, title: `NO PAIRING THIS TIME`,                onScreen: `pairing needs equal steps — we need a new trick` },
    { n:  4, afterSeg:  5, title: `LAST TIME • FIND THE SUM`,            onScreen: `5 + 8 + 11 + … + 47 · n = 15` },
    { n:  5, afterSeg:  6, title: `WHAT YOU ALREADY HAVE`,               onScreen: `a = 5 · l = 47 · n = 15` },
    { n:  6, afterSeg:  8, title: `SUBSTITUTE`,                          onScreen: `S₁₅ = 15/2 (5 + 47)` },
    { n:  7, afterSeg:  9, title: `HALVE THE 52 FIRST`,                  onScreen: `5 + 47 = 52 → 52 ÷ 2 = 26 → 15 × 26` },
    { n:  8, afterSeg: 10, title: `ANSWER • 390`,                        onScreen: `S₁₅ = 390 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n:  9, afterSeg: 12, title: `A SMALL GEOMETRIC SERIES`,            onScreen: `2 + 4 + 8` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg: 13, title: `JUST ADD THEM • 14`,                  onScreen: `2 + 4 = 6 → 6 + 8 = 14` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg: 15, title: `THE OLD FORMULA FAILS`,               onScreen: `red · 3/2 (2 + 8) = 15, but the sum is 14` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg: 17, title: `THE PAIRS ARE NOT EQUAL`,             onScreen: `2 + 8 = 10, so the middle should be 5 — but it is 4` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg: 18, title: `PAIRING NEEDS EQUAL STEPS`,           onScreen: `arithmetic pairs · geometric does not` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg: 19, title: `THE TRICK`,                           onScreen: `multiply by r, then subtract` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg: 21, title: `CALL THE TOTAL S`,                    onScreen: `S = 2 + 4 + 8 + 16` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg: 23, title: `MULTIPLY EVERY TERM BY 2`,            onScreen: `2S = 4 + 8 + 16 + 32` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg: 25, title: `SUBTRACT THE LINES`,                  onScreen: `the middle terms cancel` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg: 26, title: `ONLY THE ENDS SURVIVE`,               onScreen: `2S − S = 32 − 2` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg: 27, title: `S = 30`,                              onScreen: `S = 30 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg: 28, title: `CHECK BY ADDING`,                     onScreen: `2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 30 ✓` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg: 30, title: `NOW WITH LETTERS`,                    onScreen: `S = a + ar + ar² + … + arⁿ⁻¹` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg: 31, title: `MULTIPLY BY R`,                       onScreen: `rS = ar + ar² + … + arⁿ` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg: 32, title: `SUBTRACT • THE MIDDLE CANCELS`,       onScreen: `rS − S = arⁿ − a` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg: 33, title: `FACTORISE BOTH SIDES`,                onScreen: `S(r − 1) = a(rⁿ − 1)` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg: 34, title: `THE FORMULA`,                         onScreen: `Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg: 35, title: `THE SECOND VERSION`,                  onScreen: `Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r)` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg: 36, title: `SAME FORMULA TURNED AROUND`,          onScreen: `r > 1 vs r < 1 — both on the formula sheet` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg: 37, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • NOVEMBER 2014`,         onScreen: `NSC Mathematics P1 · November 2014 · Question 3.2.2` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg: 38, title: `THE QUESTION • SUM OF 10 TERMS`,      onScreen: `3.2.2 Calculate the sum of the first 10 terms. (2)` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg: 41, title: `FIND A AND R`,                        onScreen: `a = 16 · r = 4 ÷ 16 = ¼` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg: 43, title: `CHECK THE RATIO TWICE`,               onScreen: `1 ÷ 4 = ¼ — same ratio ✓` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg: 44, title: `R IS LESS THAN 1`,                    onScreen: `use Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r)` },
    { n: 33, afterSeg: 45, title: `SUBSTITUTE`,                          onScreen: `S₁₀ = 16(1 − (¼)¹⁰)/(1 − ¼)` },
    { n: 34, afterSeg: 47, title: `THE BOTTOM`,                          onScreen: `1 − ¼ = ¾` },
    { n: 35, afterSeg: 49, title: `SIXTEEN OVER THREE QUARTERS`,         onScreen: `16 ÷ ¾ = 64/3 = 21,333…` },
    { n: 36, afterSeg: 51, title: `THE TOP • ALMOST NOTHING`,            onScreen: `(¼)¹⁰ ≈ 0,00000095` },
    { n: 37, afterSeg: 54, title: `ANSWER • 21,33`,                      onScreen: `S₁₀ ≈ 21,33 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 38, afterSeg: 55, title: `HOLD THAT THOUGHT`,                   onScreen: `ten terms — and it has barely moved off 21,33` },
    { n: 39, afterSeg: 56, title: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS`,                onScreen: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS` },
    { n: 40, afterSeg: 57, title: `MARK LOSS 1 • DIVIDE FOR R`,          onScreen: `1. r = later ÷ earlier, never minus` },
    { n: 41, afterSeg: 58, title: `MARK LOSS 2 • N COUNTS TERMS`,        onScreen: `2. n counts terms` },
    { n: 42, afterSeg: 59, title: `MARK LOSS 3 • DO NOT ROUND EARLY`,    onScreen: `3. round only at the end` },
    { n: 43, afterSeg: 60, title: `MARK LOSS 4 • FRACTION RATIO`,        onScreen: `4. r < 1? use the 1 − r version` },
    { n: 44, afterSeg: 61, title: `REMEMBER`,                            onScreen: `REMEMBER` },
    { n: 45, afterSeg: 62, title: `RECAP 1 • ADD OR MULTIPLY`,           onScreen: `1. the series follows the sequence` },
    { n: 46, afterSeg: 63, title: `RECAP 2 • MULTIPLY AND SUBTRACT`,     onScreen: `2. multiply by r, then subtract` },
    { n: 47, afterSeg: 64, title: `RECAP 3 • THE FORMULA`,               onScreen: `3. Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1)` },
    { n: 48, afterSeg: 65, title: `YOUR TURN • FIND S20`,                onScreen: `16 + 4 + 1 + … Find S₂₀.` },
    { n: 49, afterSeg: 66, title: `PAUSE AND TRY`,                       onScreen: `PAUSE AND TRY` },
    { n: 50, afterSeg: 69, title: `NEXT • INFINITE GEOMETRIC SERIES`,    onScreen: `NEXT — EPISODE 7: INFINITE GEOMETRIC SERIES` },
  ],
},
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// EPISODE 7 — paste into the EPISODES array in
// scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs, after the episode 6 block
// (immediately before the closing  ];  ).
// Then:  node --env-file=.env.local scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs 7
// FIRST: unzip Episode-07-Slides.zip into podcasts/Working-It-Out/slides/ep07
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  num: 7,
  file: 'Episode 7 - Infinite Geometric Series',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode seven. Infinite geometric series.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Every episode so far has stopped somewhere. Ten terms. Two hundred and fifty one terms. Today we never stop.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `You add numbers forever, and you still get an answer?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Sometimes. And when you can, the formula is the shortest one in the whole topic. But first, your homework.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You had sixteen, plus four, plus one, and so on. I asked you for twenty terms.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Same formula as last time. Sixteen, times, one minus a quarter to the power of twenty, all divided by three quarters.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And I got twenty one comma three three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And what did you get for ten terms, last time?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Twenty one comma three three. The same. I added ten more numbers and nothing moved.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Nothing moved. That is today's whole episode.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Look at why. The terms are shrinking. Sixteen, four, one, a quarter, a sixteenth.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `By the twentieth term it is almost zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So adding it changes almost nothing. The total stops climbing. The exam word is converges.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Converges. Meaning it settles on one number.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It settles. Here is a picture. Stand two metres from a wall. Walk half way. That is one metre.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Then half of what is left. Half a metre. Then half again. A quarter.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `I get closer and closer, but I never pass the wall.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And your total distance settles on two metres. One, plus a half, plus a quarter, and so on, adds up to two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the warning. It does not always work. Take two, plus four, plus eight, plus sixteen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Those are getting bigger, not smaller.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the total runs away forever. There is no answer. The exam word is diverges.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So there is a condition. The ratio must sit between negative one and one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Because if the ratio is bigger than one, the terms grow instead of shrinking.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And between negative one and one, every term is a fraction of the one before. Writing that condition down is worth a mark on its own.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the formula, and you already have it. Last week you used a, times one minus r to the n, divided by one minus r.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Think about r to the n when the ratio is a fraction and n gets huge. A quarter to the power of ten was already almost nothing.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So a quarter to the power of a million is basically zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So cross it out. The bracket becomes one. And you are left with a, divided by one minus r.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is much shorter than I expected.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The sum to infinity is a, divided by one minus r. Now test it on your homework. First term sixteen, ratio a quarter.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Sixteen divided by three quarters. That is sixty four divided by three. Twenty one comma three three three, and it carries on.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is the number you have been creeping towards for two episodes. Now you know exactly where it was going.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now a real exam question. This one is from a different paper. November two thousand and eighteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A geometric series has a constant ratio of a half, and a sum to infinity of six. Calculate the first term.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `They gave me the answer and they want the beginning. That is backwards.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It is backwards. Write the formula down anyway.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Six equals a, divided by one minus a half. One minus a half is a half.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So six equals a divided by a half. Dividing by a half is the same as times two. So a is twelve.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Careful. Check it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `If a is twelve and the ratio is a half, the sum to infinity is twelve divided by a half. That is twenty four, not six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So you went the wrong way. Six equals a divided by a half. To get a on its own, what do you do to both sides?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Multiply both sides by a half. So a is six times a half. Three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three. Two marks. Check that one too.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three divided by a half is six. Correct.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Next part. Calculate the eighth term.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is episode two. The enth term is a, times r to the n minus one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three, times a half to the power of seven. That is three divided by one hundred and twenty eight.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two marks. And notice how small that is already. The eighth term is worth about two hundredths.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Last part, and it uses everything. Sigma, from k equals one to n, of three, times two to the power of, one minus k. The total is five comma eight one two five. Find n.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `First, is it really our series? Put k equals one. Two to the power of zero is one. So the first term is three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Put k equals two. Two to the power of negative one is a half. Three times a half is one comma five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `First term three, ratio a half. The same series.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now, is this one finite or infinite?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The top is n, not infinity. So it stops. It is finite, and I use last week's formula.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three, times one minus a half to the power of n, divided by one minus a half. The bottom is a half. And dividing by a half is the same as times two, so the three becomes six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So six, times the bracket, equals five comma eight one two five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Divide both sides by six. The bracket is zero comma nine six eight seven five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So a half to the power of n is one minus that. One divided by thirty two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And a half to the power of what gives one divided by thirty two?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two, four, eight, sixteen, thirty two. That is five halvings. So n is five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `n is five. Four marks. And look what you did. You used episode six's formula inside episode seven's question.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Four things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Check the ratio before you use the infinity formula. If it is not between negative one and one, there is no answer, and saying so earns the mark.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. The infinity formula has no n in it. If your working still has an n, you are on the wrong formula.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. When they give you the sum and want the first term, do not divide. Multiply the sum by one minus r.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number four. Sigma with a number on top is finite. Sigma with the infinity sign on top is infinite. Read the top before you choose a formula.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. A series converges when the terms shrink towards zero. That happens when the ratio is between negative one and one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. The sum to infinity is a, divided by one minus r. Two letters, and no n.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three. It comes straight from last week's formula, with r to the n crossed out, because it disappears.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is the topic. Seven episodes ago you could not find the two hundred and fifty first term. Now you can add up a list that never ends.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And it was really the same few ideas the whole way through.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Find the pattern. Find the rule. Add them up. Check your answer. That is sequences and series. Well done for finishing it.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:  0, title: `EPISODE 7 • INFINITE GEOMETRIC SERIES`, onScreen: `EPISODE 7 — INFINITE GEOMETRIC SERIES` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:  1, title: `IT ALWAYS STOPPED • TODAY IT DOES NOT`, onScreen: `until now it STOPPED · today it NEVER STOPS` },
    { n:  3, afterSeg:  4, title: `LAST TIME • FIND S20`,                  onScreen: `16 + 4 + 1 + … find S₂₀` },
    { n:  4, afterSeg:  5, title: `SAME FORMULA AS LAST WEEK`,             onScreen: `S₂₀ = 16(1 − (¼)²⁰)/(¾)` },
    { n:  5, afterSeg:  6, title: `ANSWER • 21-33 AGAIN`,                  onScreen: `S₂₀ ≈ 21,33 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n:  6, afterSeg:  8, title: `TEN TERMS VERSUS TWENTY`,               onScreen: `S₁₀ ≈ 21,33 · S₂₀ ≈ 21,33 — nothing moved` },
    { n:  7, afterSeg: 10, title: `THE TERMS SHRINK`,                      onScreen: `16 → 4 → 1 → ¼ → 1/16` },
    { n:  8, afterSeg: 11, title: `SHRINKING TOWARDS ZERO`,                onScreen: `adding another changes almost nothing` },
    { n:  9, afterSeg: 12, title: `CONVERGES`,                             onScreen: `CONVERGES — the total settles on one number` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg: 15, title: `WALKING TO A WALL`,                     onScreen: `1 m → ½ m → ¼ m …` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg: 17, title: `IT SETTLES ON 2 METRES`,                onScreen: `1 + ½ + ¼ + … = 2 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg: 18, title: `BUT NOT ALWAYS`,                        onScreen: `2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + …` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg: 20, title: `DIVERGES`,                              onScreen: `DIVERGES — the total runs away, no answer` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg: 21, title: `THE CONDITION`,                         onScreen: `−1 < r < 1 (boxed)` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg: 23, title: `WHY THE CONDITION WORKS`,               onScreen: `r > 1 grows · −1 < r < 1 shrinks` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg: 24, title: `START FROM LAST WEEK`,                  onScreen: `Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r)` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg: 26, title: `R TO THE N DISAPPEARS`,                 onScreen: `as n → ∞, rⁿ → 0` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg: 27, title: `SO CROSS IT OUT`,                       onScreen: `the bracket becomes 1` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg: 28, title: `THE SUM TO INFINITY`,                   onScreen: `S∞ = a/(1 − r) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg: 30, title: `TEST IT ON YOUR OWN SERIES`,            onScreen: `S∞ = 16 ÷ ¾ = 64/3 = 21,333…` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg: 31, title: `THAT IS WHERE IT WAS GOING`,            onScreen: `21,33 was 64/3 all along` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg: 32, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • NOVEMBER 2018`,           onScreen: `NSC Mathematics P1 · November 2018 · Question 3` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg: 33, title: `THE QUESTION 3-1`,                      onScreen: `3.1 r = ½ and S∞ = 6. Calculate the first term. (2)` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg: 36, title: `WRITE THE FORMULA FIRST`,               onScreen: `6 = a ÷ (1 − ½)` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg: 37, title: `TYLA DIVIDES INSTEAD`,                onScreen: `red · a = 6 × 2 = 12` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg: 39, title: `CHECK IT • IT FAILS`,                   onScreen: `red · 12 ÷ ½ = 24, but S∞ must be 6` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg: 41, title: `MULTIPLY BOTH SIDES INSTEAD`,           onScreen: `a = 6 × ½` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg: 42, title: `ANSWER • A EQUALS 3`,                   onScreen: `a = 3 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg: 43, title: `CHECK IT`,                              onScreen: `3 ÷ ½ = 6 ✓` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg: 44, title: `THE QUESTION 3-2`,                      onScreen: `3.2 Calculate the 8th term. (2)` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg: 46, title: `T8 EQUALS 3 OVER 128`,                  onScreen: `T₈ = 3(½)⁷ = 3/128 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg: 47, title: `ALREADY ALMOST NOTHING`,                onScreen: `3/128 ≈ 0,023` },
    { n: 33, afterSeg: 48, title: `THE QUESTION 3-3`,                      onScreen: `3.3 ∑ from k = 1 to n of 3(2)^(1−k) = 5,8125. Find n. (4)` },
    { n: 34, afterSeg: 50, title: `IS IT REALLY OUR SERIES`,               onScreen: `k = 1 → 3 · k = 2 → 1,5` },
    { n: 35, afterSeg: 51, title: `THE SAME SERIES`,                       onScreen: `a = 3 · r = ½ ✓` },
    { n: 36, afterSeg: 53, title: `FINITE OR INFINITE`,                    onScreen: `top is n → finite · top is ∞ → infinite` },
    { n: 37, afterSeg: 54, title: `USE LAST WEEK FORMULA`,                 onScreen: `Sₙ = 6[1 − (½)ⁿ]` },
    { n: 38, afterSeg: 55, title: `SET IT EQUAL TO THE TOTAL`,             onScreen: `6[1 − (½)ⁿ] = 5,8125` },
    { n: 39, afterSeg: 56, title: `DIVIDE BOTH SIDES BY 6`,                onScreen: `1 − (½)ⁿ = 0,96875` },
    { n: 40, afterSeg: 57, title: `MAKE THE POWER THE SUBJECT`,            onScreen: `(½)ⁿ = 1/32` },
    { n: 41, afterSeg: 59, title: `ANSWER • N EQUALS 5`,                   onScreen: `n = 5 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 42, afterSeg: 60, title: `EPISODE 6 INSIDE EPISODE 7`,            onScreen: `it looked infinite — it was finite` },
    { n: 43, afterSeg: 61, title: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS`,                  onScreen: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS` },
    { n: 44, afterSeg: 62, title: `MARK LOSS 1 • CHECK THE RATIO`,         onScreen: `1. check −1 < r < 1 first` },
    { n: 45, afterSeg: 63, title: `MARK LOSS 2 • NO N`,                    onScreen: `2. no n in the infinity formula` },
    { n: 46, afterSeg: 64, title: `MARK LOSS 3 • MULTIPLY`,                onScreen: `3. a = S∞ × (1 − r)` },
    { n: 47, afterSeg: 65, title: `MARK LOSS 4 • READ THE TOP`,            onScreen: `4. read the top of the sigma` },
    { n: 48, afterSeg: 66, title: `REMEMBER`,                              onScreen: `REMEMBER` },
    { n: 49, afterSeg: 67, title: `RECAP 1 • IT CONVERGES`,                onScreen: `1. terms shrink → it converges` },
    { n: 50, afterSeg: 68, title: `RECAP 2 • THE FORMULA`,                 onScreen: `2. S∞ = a/(1 − r)` },
    { n: 51, afterSeg: 69, title: `RECAP 3 • WHERE IT COMES FROM`,         onScreen: `3. last week's formula with rⁿ gone` },
    { n: 52, afterSeg: 70, title: `SEVEN EPISODES`,                        onScreen: `SEQUENCES & SERIES — seven episodes` },
    { n: 53, afterSeg: 72, title: `THE WHOLE TOPIC`,                       onScreen: `find the pattern · find the rule · add them up · check` },
  ],
},
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// REVISION PART 1 — EPISODE 1: Algebraic Products
// Paste into the EPISODES array in scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs
// (immediately before the closing  ];  ).
// FIRST: unzip Revision-1-Episode-01-Slides.zip into
//        podcasts/Working-It-Out/Revision-Part-1/slides/ep01
// Then:  node --env-file=.env.local scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs 1
//        (the `series` field keeps it away from Sequences episode 1)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  num: 1,
  series: 'Revision-Part-1',
  file: 'Episode 1 - Algebraic Products',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Revision, part one. Algebra, exponents and surds. Episode one. Algebraic products.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `This is the start of our revision series. These are the skills that Grade twelve assumes you already have.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And if you do not have them?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Then question one of your matric paper is very hard, for no good reason. So we fix that first.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Today, products. A product just means the answer you get when you multiply.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So an algebraic product is what you get when you multiply expressions together.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Yes. And there is one rule for all of them. Every term in the first bracket must multiply every term in the second bracket.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Every term, times every term. Nothing gets left out.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Nothing gets left out. That is the whole topic. Let us do three of them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `First one. Bracket, x plus three, bracket, times, bracket, x minus five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x times x is x squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x times negative five is negative five x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three times x is three x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And three times negative five is negative fifteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Watch the signs there. A positive times a negative always gives you a negative.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now collect the middle two. And you are only allowed to add them because they are both x terms. Same letter, same power.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative five x, plus three x. Starting at negative five and going up three gives negative two x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x squared, minus two x, minus fifteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two brackets with an x in each gives you a quadratic. An x squared at the front, every time.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Second one, and this is the one people rush. Two x minus one, times, x squared plus three x minus four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Now the second bracket has three terms.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So there are six multiplications, not four. And there is one rule you need before we start.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `When you multiply the same letter, you add the powers. x on its own is really x to the power one. So x times x squared is x to the power three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One power, plus two powers, gives three powers. That makes sense.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Take the two x first.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two x times x squared is two x cubed. Two x times three x is six x squared. Two x times negative four is negative eight x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the negative one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative one times x squared is negative x squared. Negative one times three x is negative three x. And negative one times negative four is positive four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two negatives make a positive. That sign is worth checking every single time.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now collect the like terms. Only the x squareds go together, and only the x terms go together.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Six x squared minus x squared is five x squared. Negative eight x minus three x is negative eleven x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So two x cubed, plus five x squared, minus eleven x, plus four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A linear bracket times a quadratic bracket gives a cubic. An x cubed at the front.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Third one. And this is where marks disappear. Bracket, x minus four, bracket, squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Squared. So, x squared minus sixteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test it. Put x equals one into your answer.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One, minus sixteen. Negative fifteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now put x equals one into the original. One minus four, and then squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative three, squared. That is positive nine.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Nine is not negative fifteen. So I am wrong.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You are. Squaring a bracket does not square the terms separately. Write it out as two brackets and use the rule.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x minus four, times, x minus four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x squared, minus four x, minus four x, plus sixteen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x squared, minus eight x, plus sixteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And test it again. x equals one gives one, minus eight, plus sixteen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Nine. It matches now.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `There are three of these worth knowing by sight. They come up constantly.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `a plus b, squared, is a squared, plus two a b, plus b squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Where does the two a b come from?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Write it as two brackets and you will see. a times b gives you a b. And b times a gives you a b again. Two lots of a b.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `a minus b, squared, is a squared, minus two a b, plus b squared.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And a plus b, times a minus b, is a squared minus b squared. There you get plus a b and minus a b, so the middle cancels.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So only the last one loses its middle term.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Only that one. The other two keep it, and forgetting it is the mistake you just made.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now, does this actually appear in matric? Here is November two thousand and sixteen, question one point three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x and y, if x equals two y, and x squared minus five x y equals negative twenty four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That looks like simultaneous equations, not products.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It is. But look what happens when you substitute. Replace every x with two y.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Bracket two y bracket, squared, minus five, times two y, times y.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And now you are expanding. Careful with that first part.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two y, squared, means two y times two y. Two times two is four, and y times y is y squared. So it is four y squared.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the second part?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Five times two is ten. And y times y is y squared. So that is ten y squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So four y squared minus ten y squared is negative six y squared, and that equals negative twenty four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Divide both sides by negative six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `y squared equals four. So y is two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Careful. What else, squared, gives you four?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative two. Negative two, squared, is also four. So y is two, or negative two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Both of them. Miss the negative one and you lose a mark. And x is two y, so x is four, or negative four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is a four mark question, and the only hard part was expanding correctly.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Every term times every term. Two terms times three terms is six multiplications.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. Squaring a bracket is not squaring the terms. Write it out as two brackets first.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. When you square something like two y, the number gets squared too. Two y, squared, is four y squared.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. A linear bracket times a linear bracket gives a quadratic. Times a quadratic bracket gives a cubic.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. If you are not sure, test your answer with a number. Put x equals one into both, and see if they match.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question. Expand, x plus five, times, x minus two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `I will give you the answer next time. And next time we do the same thing backwards.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Backwards?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Today we multiplied brackets out. Next time we take an expression and put the brackets back. That is factorising, and it is episode two.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:  0, title: `REVISION 1 • EPISODE 1 • ALGEBRAIC PRODUCTS`, onScreen: `EPISODE 1 — ALGEBRAIC PRODUCTS` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:  1, title: `WHAT REVISION PART 1 IS`,          onScreen: `the skills Grade 12 assumes you have` },
    { n:  3, afterSeg:  3, title: `QUESTION 1 GETS EASIER`,           onScreen: `~25 marks of Paper 1 live here` },
    { n:  4, afterSeg:  4, title: `A PRODUCT`,                        onScreen: `the answer you get when you multiply` },
    { n:  5, afterSeg:  6, title: `THE ONE RULE`,                     onScreen: `every term × every term` },
    { n:  6, afterSeg:  8, title: `EXAMPLE 1`,                        onScreen: `(x + 3)(x − 5)` },
    { n:  7, afterSeg: 10, title: `START WITH THE X`,                 onScreen: `x × x = x² · x × (−5) = −5x` },
    { n:  8, afterSeg: 13, title: `NOW THE 3`,                        onScreen: `3 × x = 3x · 3 × (−5) = −15` },
    { n:  9, afterSeg: 14, title: `THE SIGN RULES`,                   onScreen: `+ × − → negative · − × − → positive` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg: 15, title: `LIKE TERMS`,                       onScreen: `same letter, same power` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg: 16, title: `COLLECT THE MIDDLE TWO`,           onScreen: `−5x + 3x = −2x` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg: 17, title: `ANSWER 1`,                         onScreen: `x² − 2x − 15 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg: 18, title: `LINEAR TIMES LINEAR`,              onScreen: `→ a quadratic` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg: 19, title: `EXAMPLE 2`,                        onScreen: `(2x − 1)(x² + 3x − 4)` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg: 21, title: `SIX MULTIPLICATIONS`,              onScreen: `2 terms × 3 terms = six, not four` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg: 22, title: `ADD THE POWERS`,                   onScreen: `x is really x¹` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg: 23, title: `WHY X TIMES X SQUARED IS X CUBED`, onScreen: `1 + 2 = 3` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg: 25, title: `TAKE THE 2X FIRST`,                onScreen: `2x³ · 6x² · −8x` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg: 27, title: `NOW THE MINUS 1`,                  onScreen: `−x² · −3x · +4` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg: 28, title: `TWO NEGATIVES MAKE A POSITIVE`,    onScreen: `−1 × −4 = +4` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg: 29, title: `COLLECT LIKE TERMS`,               onScreen: `5x² · −11x` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg: 31, title: `ANSWER 2`,                         onScreen: `2x³ + 5x² − 11x + 4 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg: 32, title: `LINEAR TIMES QUADRATIC`,           onScreen: `→ a cubic` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg: 33, title: `EXAMPLE 3`,                        onScreen: `(x − 4)²` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg: 34, title: `TYLA SQUARES EACH TERM`,         onScreen: `red · x² − 16` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg: 36, title: `TEST IT • PUT X EQUALS 1`,         onScreen: `red · = −15` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg: 38, title: `THE ORIGINAL AT X EQUALS 1`,       onScreen: `(−3)² = 9` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg: 39, title: `IT DOES NOT MATCH`,                onScreen: `red · 9 ≠ −15 ✗` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg: 41, title: `WRITE IT AS TWO BRACKETS`,         onScreen: `(x − 4)(x − 4)` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg: 42, title: `EVERY TERM TIMES EVERY TERM`,      onScreen: `x² − 4x − 4x + 16` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg: 43, title: `ANSWER 3`,                         onScreen: `x² − 8x + 16 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg: 45, title: `TEST IT AGAIN`,                    onScreen: `= 9 ✓ it matches` },
    { n: 33, afterSeg: 47, title: `SPECIAL PRODUCT 1`,                onScreen: `(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²` },
    { n: 34, afterSeg: 49, title: `WHERE THE 2AB COMES FROM`,         onScreen: `ab from each side → 2ab` },
    { n: 35, afterSeg: 50, title: `SPECIAL PRODUCT 2`,                onScreen: `(a − b)² = a² − 2ab + b²` },
    { n: 36, afterSeg: 51, title: `SPECIAL PRODUCT 3`,                onScreen: `+ab and −ab cancel → a² − b²` },
    { n: 37, afterSeg: 53, title: `ONLY THE THIRD LOSES ITS MIDDLE`,  onScreen: `the two squares keep it` },
    { n: 38, afterSeg: 54, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • NOVEMBER 2016`,      onScreen: `NSC Mathematics P1 · Nov 2016 · Q1.3` },
    { n: 39, afterSeg: 55, title: `THE QUESTION 1-3`,                 onScreen: `x = 2y and x² − 5xy = −24. (4)` },
    { n: 40, afterSeg: 58, title: `SUBSTITUTE`,                       onScreen: `(2y)² − 5(2y)(y) = −24` },
    { n: 41, afterSeg: 60, title: `THE 2 GETS SQUARED TOO`,           onScreen: `(2y)² = 4y²` },
    { n: 42, afterSeg: 62, title: `NOW THE SECOND PART`,              onScreen: `5(2y)(y) = 10y²` },
    { n: 43, afterSeg: 63, title: `PUT IT TOGETHER`,                  onScreen: `−6y² = −24` },
    { n: 44, afterSeg: 64, title: `DIVIDE BOTH SIDES BY MINUS 6`,     onScreen: `y² = 4` },
    { n: 45, afterSeg: 66, title: `WHAT ELSE SQUARED GIVES 4`,        onScreen: `(−2)² = 4 as well` },
    { n: 46, afterSeg: 67, title: `BOTH VALUES OF Y`,                 onScreen: `y = 2 or y = −2 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 47, afterSeg: 68, title: `ANSWER • BOTH PAIRS`,              onScreen: `(4 ; 2) or (−4 ; −2) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 48, afterSeg: 69, title: `FOUR MARKS`,                       onScreen: `the only hard part was expanding` },
    { n: 49, afterSeg: 70, title: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS`,             onScreen: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS` },
    { n: 50, afterSeg: 71, title: `MARK LOSS 1 • EVERY TERM`,         onScreen: `1. six multiplications, not four` },
    { n: 51, afterSeg: 72, title: `MARK LOSS 2 • SQUARING A BRACKET`, onScreen: `2. write it out as two brackets` },
    { n: 52, afterSeg: 73, title: `MARK LOSS 3 • SQUARE THE NUMBER`,  onScreen: `3. (2y)² = 4y², never 2y²` },
    { n: 53, afterSeg: 74, title: `REMEMBER`,                         onScreen: `REMEMBER` },
    { n: 54, afterSeg: 75, title: `RECAP 1 • THE ANSWER SHAPE`,       onScreen: `1. linear × linear → quadratic` },
    { n: 55, afterSeg: 76, title: `RECAP 2 • TEST WITH A NUMBER`,     onScreen: `2. put x = 1 into both` },
    { n: 56, afterSeg: 77, title: `YOUR TURN`,                        onScreen: `Expand (x + 5)(x − 2)` },
    { n: 57, afterSeg: 78, title: `PAUSE AND TRY`,                    onScreen: `PAUSE AND TRY` },
    { n: 58, afterSeg: 81, title: `NEXT • FACTORISING`,               onScreen: `NEXT — EPISODE 2: FACTORISING` },
  ],
},
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// REVISION PART 1 — EPISODE 2: Factorising
// Paste into the EPISODES array in scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs,
// next to the existing series: 'Revision-Part-1' episode 1 object.
// Slides: unzip into podcasts/Working-It-Out/Revision-Part-1/slides/ep02
// Run:    node --env-file=.env.local scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs Revision-Part-1:2
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  num: 2,
  series: 'Revision-Part-1',
  file: 'Episode 2 - Factorising',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode two. Factorising.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Last time we multiplied brackets out. Today we put them back.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `First, my homework.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You had, bracket x plus five, bracket, times, bracket x minus two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x times x is x squared. And x times negative two is negative two x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Five times x is five x. And five times negative two is negative ten.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now collect the middle two. They are both x terms, so you are allowed to add them.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative two x, plus five x. Starting at negative two and going up five gives positive three x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x squared, plus three x, minus ten.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Correct. And now I am going to take your own answer and hand it straight back to you.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Factorise x squared plus three x minus ten.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `But that is where I started. It is x plus five, times x minus two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It is. And that is the whole of today. Factorising is episode one, read backwards.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So the answer to a factorising question is always brackets.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Always. Which means you can always check yourself. Multiply your brackets out again. If you do not land back on the question, you are wrong.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now, one habit before you start any of these. Count the terms.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two terms, you are looking at squares or cubes. Three terms, it is a trinomial. Four terms, you group them in pairs.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And is there anything before that?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One thing. A common factor. Look for something that divides into every term, and take it out first. Every single time.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `First one. Factorise fully. Two x squared, minus eighteen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two terms. So squares or cubes.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Before that. Is there a common factor?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two goes into two x squared, and two goes into eighteen. So I can take out a two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Take it out. What is left inside?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two x squared divided by two is x squared. And eighteen divided by two is nine. So, two, bracket, x squared minus nine.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now look inside that bracket. x squared minus nine. Two terms, both of them perfect squares, with a minus between them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is the third special product from last time, backwards. a squared minus b squared is a plus b, times a minus b.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So a is x. And b is three, because three squared is nine.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x squared minus nine is x plus three, times x minus three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Put the whole thing together.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two, times x plus three, times x minus three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is what fully means. You keep going until nothing inside any bracket can be factorised again.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `How do I know when I am finished?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Look at every bracket you have written. If one of them is still two squares with a minus, or still a trinomial, you are not finished.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One warning while we are here. x squared plus nine does not factorise. A plus between two squares does nothing.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So only the minus one works.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Only the minus. Second one, and this is straight out of matric. November two thousand and seventeen, question one point one point one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x. x squared, plus nine x, plus fourteen, equals zero. Three marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three terms. So it is a trinomial.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And there is no common factor. Nothing divides into one, nine and fourteen. So go straight to the brackets.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You need two numbers that multiply to give fourteen, and add to give nine.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Fourteen is one times fourteen, or two times seven.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One and fourteen add to fifteen. Two and seven add to nine. So it is two and seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Write your brackets.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x plus seven, times x plus two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Check the middle term before you go any further. Seven x, plus two x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Nine x. That matches the question.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the part people forget. The question said solve, not factorise. So you are not finished.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `If two things multiply together and give you zero, one of them has to be zero. There is no other way to make zero by multiplying.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So either x plus seven is zero, or x plus two is zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x equals negative seven, or x equals negative two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Both of them. And watch that sign. The bracket says plus seven, but the answer says negative seven.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Because I take the seven to the other side.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Exactly. Three marks. One for the factors, and one for each answer.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Third one. And this is where most of the marks in this topic go. Factorise three x squared, minus five x, minus two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three terms again. So I need two numbers that multiply to negative two and add to negative five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Try it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative two and one multiply to negative two. But they add to negative one, not negative five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the rule you used a minute ago does not work here. Why not?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Because of the three in front of the x squared?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Because of the three. When the number in front of x squared is not one, those two numbers do not simply drop into the brackets.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Show me what you would have written anyway.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three x minus two, times x plus one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now check it. Multiply it out.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three x times x is three x squared. Three x times one is three x. Negative two times x is negative two x. And negative two times one is negative two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three x, minus two x, is plus x. So I get three x squared, plus x, minus two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the question says minus five x. So it is wrong. But look carefully at what you have. The right numbers, in the wrong places.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I move them?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Swap them and try again. Put the two with the x, and the one with the three x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three x plus one, times x minus two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Check the middle again.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three x times negative two is negative six x. And one times x is x. Negative six x, plus x, is negative five x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That matches. So it is three x plus one, times x minus two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is the method. When there is a number in front, you try an arrangement and you check the middle term. It is not guessing. There are only a few arrangements, and the middle term tells you straight away which one is right.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two more you should know by sight. Four terms, and cubes.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Four terms. Factorise fully. x cubed, plus three x squared, minus four x, minus twelve.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Take the first two together, and the last two together.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The first two both have an x squared in them. x cubed plus three x squared is x squared, bracket, x plus three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The last two both have a four. Negative four x minus twelve is negative four, bracket, x plus three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now look at your two brackets.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `They are the same. Both of them say x plus three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is the whole trick. When the two brackets match, you take that bracket out.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So, x plus three, times x squared minus four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Are you finished?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `No. x squared minus four is two squares with a minus. So it is x minus two, times x plus two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So it is x plus three, times x minus two, times x plus two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now cubes. Two terms, and both of them are perfect cubes.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `x cubed minus eight is x minus two, times, x squared plus two x plus four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Where does that second bracket come from?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The small bracket is just the cube roots. x, and two. The big bracket is built out of those same two: the first one squared, then the two of them multiplied, then the second one squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And the signs?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The small bracket keeps the sign of the question. In the big bracket the middle sign flips, and the last sign is always a plus.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So x cubed plus twenty seven is x plus three, times, x squared minus three x plus nine.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Plus in the small bracket, minus in the middle, plus at the end.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Every time. Now, three things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Common factor first. Always. It makes everything after it smaller and easier.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. Fully means fully. If a bracket still has two squares and a minus in it, you are not finished.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. Check the middle term. Multiply your brackets out again. It takes five seconds and it catches almost every mistake.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. Factorising is expanding, backwards. The answer is always brackets, and you can always check it yourself.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. Count the terms. Two terms, squares or cubes. Three terms, a trinomial. Four terms, group them in pairs.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question. Factorise fully. Two x squared, plus ten x, plus twelve.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `I will give you the answer next time.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And what is next time?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now that you can expand and factorise, we start solving. Episode three. Linear equations.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:   0, title: `REVISION 1 • EPISODE 2 • FACTORISING`, onScreen: `EPISODE 2 — FACTORISING` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:   1, title: `LAST TIME WE TOOK THE BRACKETS AWAY`, onScreen: `today we put them back` },
    { n:  3, afterSeg:   3, title: `YOUR HOMEWORK`, onScreen: `(x + 5)(x − 2) · expand and simplify` },
    { n:  4, afterSeg:   4, title: `EVERY TERM × EVERY TERM`, onScreen: `x × (−2) = −2x` },
    { n:  5, afterSeg:   5, title: `NOW THE 5`, onScreen: `5 × (−2) = −10` },
    { n:  6, afterSeg:   7, title: `COLLECT THE MIDDLE TWO`, onScreen: `−2x + 5x = +3x` },
    { n:  7, afterSeg:   8, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `x² + 3x − 10 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n:  8, afterSeg:  10, title: `NOW THE SAME THING BACKWARDS`, onScreen: `x² + 3x − 10 · factorise` },
    { n:  9, afterSeg:  11, title: `THE ANSWER IS WHERE SHE STARTED`, onScreen: `(x + 5)(x − 2) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg:  12, title: `FACTORISING`, onScreen: `expanding takes brackets away · factorising puts them back` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg:  14, title: `YOU CAN ALWAYS CHECK YOURSELF`, onScreen: `the one topic that marks its own work` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg:  15, title: `FIRST, COUNT THE TERMS`, onScreen: `FIRST, COUNT THE TERMS` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg:  16, title: `HOW MANY TERMS ARE THERE?`, onScreen: `2 terms → squares or cubes · 3 terms → a trinomial · 4 terms → group them in pairs` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg:  18, title: `BUT BEFORE ANY OF THAT`, onScreen: `every single time` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg:  19, title: `EXAMPLE 1`, onScreen: `2x² − 18 · factorise fully` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg:  22, title: `IS THERE A COMMON FACTOR?`, onScreen: `so take out the 2` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg:  24, title: `WHAT IS LEFT INSIDE`, onScreen: `2(x² − 9)` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg:  25, title: `NOW LOOK INSIDE THE BRACKET`, onScreen: `two squares, with a minus between them` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg:  26, title: `FROM EPISODE 1, READ BACKWARDS`, onScreen: `a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b)` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg:  28, title: `SO A IS X AND B IS 3`, onScreen: `x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3)` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg:  30, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `2(x + 3)(x − 3) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg:  31, title: `FULLY`, onScreen: `still two squares with a minus? still a trinomial? then you are not finished` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg:  34, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `x² − 9 → (x + 3)(x − 3) · x² + 9 → does not factorise` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg:  35, title: `ONLY THE MINUS ONE WORKS`, onScreen: `a plus between two squares does nothing` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg:  36, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • NOVEMBER 2017`, onScreen: `NSC Mathematics P1 · November 2017 · Question 1.1.1 · 3 marks` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg:  37, title: `QUESTION 1.1.1`, onScreen: `x² + 9x + 14 = 0 · Solve for x.` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg:  39, title: `THREE TERMS — A TRINOMIAL`, onScreen: `so go straight to the brackets` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg:  40, title: `YOU NEED TWO NUMBERS`, onScreen: `and add to give 9` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg:  42, title: `LIST THE PAIRS THAT MAKE 14`, onScreen: `2 × 7 = 14 → 2 + 7 = 9 ✓` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg:  44, title: `WRITE THE BRACKETS`, onScreen: `(x + 7)(x + 2)` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg:  46, title: `CHECK THE MIDDLE TERM`, onScreen: `= 9x ✓ it matches` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg:  47, title: `IT SAID SOLVE, NOT FACTORISE`, onScreen: `The brackets are only the first mark. You still have to find x.` },
    { n: 33, afterSeg:  48, title: `THE ZERO RULE`, onScreen: `there is no other way to make zero by multiplying` },
    { n: 34, afterSeg:  50, title: `SO SPLIT IT INTO TWO`, onScreen: `x = −7 or x = −2 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 35, afterSeg:  52, title: `WHY THE SIGN FLIPS`, onScreen: `x = −7` },
    { n: 36, afterSeg:  53, title: `THREE MARKS`, onScreen: `miss the second answer and you lose a third of the question` },
    { n: 37, afterSeg:  54, title: `EXAMPLE 3`, onScreen: `3x² − 5x − 2 · where the marks disappear` },
    { n: 38, afterSeg:  55, title: `TYLA USES THE SAME RULE`, onScreen: `? × ? = −2 and ? + ? = −5` },
    { n: 39, afterSeg:  57, title: `THE ONLY PAIR SHE CAN FIND`, onScreen: `red · −2 + 1 = −1, not −5 ✗` },
    { n: 40, afterSeg:  60, title: `THE 3 CHANGES EVERYTHING`, onScreen: `when the number in front of x² is not 1, the two numbers do not just drop into the brackets` },
    { n: 41, afterSeg:  62, title: `SHE WRITES IT ANYWAY`, onScreen: `red · (3x − 2)(x + 1)` },
    { n: 42, afterSeg:  64, title: `CHECK IT — MULTIPLY HER BRACKETS OUT`, onScreen: `−2 × 1 = −2` },
    { n: 43, afterSeg:  65, title: `COLLECT HER MIDDLE TERM`, onScreen: `red · she gets 3x² + x − 2` },
    { n: 44, afterSeg:  66, title: `NOW COMPARE IT WITH THE QUESTION`, onScreen: `red · +x ≠ −5x ✗` },
    { n: 45, afterSeg:  67, title: `RIGHT NUMBERS, WRONG PLACES`, onScreen: `so swap them and check again` },
    { n: 46, afterSeg:  69, title: `PUT THE 2 WITH THE X`, onScreen: `(3x + 1)(x − 2)` },
    { n: 47, afterSeg:  71, title: `CHECK THE MIDDLE AGAIN`, onScreen: `−6x + x = −5x ✓` },
    { n: 48, afterSeg:  72, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `(3x + 1)(x − 2) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 49, afterSeg:  73, title: `THIS IS NOT GUESSING`, onScreen: `try it · check the middle · swap if it is wrong` },
    { n: 50, afterSeg:  74, title: `TWO MORE TO KNOW BY SIGHT`, onScreen: `TWO MORE TO KNOW BY SIGHT` },
    { n: 51, afterSeg:  75, title: `FOUR TERMS`, onScreen: `x³ + 3x² − 4x − 12 · factorise fully` },
    { n: 52, afterSeg:  76, title: `TAKE THEM IN PAIRS`, onScreen: `x³ + 3x² | −4x − 12` },
    { n: 53, afterSeg:  77, title: `THE FIRST TWO`, onScreen: `x³ + 3x² = x²(x + 3)` },
    { n: 54, afterSeg:  78, title: `NOW THE LAST TWO`, onScreen: `−4x − 12 = −4(x + 3)` },
    { n: 55, afterSeg:  80, title: `THE TWO BRACKETS ARE THE SAME`, onScreen: `both of them say (x + 3)` },
    { n: 56, afterSeg:  82, title: `SO TAKE THAT BRACKET OUT`, onScreen: `(x + 3)(x² − 4)` },
    { n: 57, afterSeg:  84, title: `NOT FINISHED — LOOK AT X² − 4`, onScreen: `x² − 4 = (x − 2)(x + 2)` },
    { n: 58, afterSeg:  85, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `(x + 3)(x − 2)(x + 2) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 59, afterSeg:  86, title: `TWO TERMS, AND BOTH ARE PERFECT CUBES`, onScreen: `x³ is a cube · and 8 is a cube, because 2³ = 8` },
    { n: 60, afterSeg:  87, title: `DIFFERENCE OF CUBES`, onScreen: `x³ − 8 = (x − 2)(x² + 2x + 4)` },
    { n: 61, afterSeg:  89, title: `WHERE THE BIG BRACKET COMES FROM`, onScreen: `x² + 2x + 4` },
    { n: 62, afterSeg:  91, title: `THE THREE SIGNS`, onScreen: `small bracket keeps the sign · middle sign flips · last sign is always a plus` },
    { n: 63, afterSeg:  92, title: `SUM OF CUBES`, onScreen: `x³ + 27 = (x + 3)(x² − 3x + 9)` },
    { n: 64, afterSeg:  94, title: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS`, onScreen: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS` },
    { n: 65, afterSeg:  95, title: `POINT 1 • COMMON FACTOR FIRST — ALWAYS`, onScreen: `Common factor first — always — it makes everything after it smaller and easier` },
    { n: 66, afterSeg:  96, title: `POINT 2 • FULLY MEANS FULLY`, onScreen: `Fully means fully — a bracket still holding two squares and a minus is not finished` },
    { n: 67, afterSeg:  97, title: `POINT 3 • CHECK THE MIDDLE TERM`, onScreen: `Check the middle term — multiply your brackets out again — five seconds, and it catches almost everything` },
    { n: 68, afterSeg:  98, title: `REMEMBER`, onScreen: `REMEMBER` },
    { n: 69, afterSeg:  99, title: `POINT 1 • FACTORISING IS EXPANDING, BACKWARDS`, onScreen: `Factorising is expanding, backwards — the answer is always brackets, and you can always check it yourself` },
    { n: 70, afterSeg: 100, title: `POINT 2 • COUNT THE TERMS`, onScreen: `Count the terms — 2 → squares or cubes · 3 → a trinomial · 4 → group them in pairs` },
    { n: 71, afterSeg: 101, title: `YOUR TURN`, onScreen: `2x² + 10x + 12 · factorise fully` },
    { n: 72, afterSeg: 102, title: `PAUSE AND TRY`, onScreen: `PAUSE AND TRY` },
    { n: 73, afterSeg: 105, title: `NEXT • LINEAR EQUATIONS`, onScreen: `NEXT — EPISODE 3: LINEAR EQUATIONS` },
  ],
},
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// REVISION PART 1 — EPISODE 3: Equations — Linear
// Paste into the EPISODES array in scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs,
// next to the existing series: 'Revision-Part-1' episode 1 and 2 objects.
// Slides: unzip into podcasts/Working-It-Out/Revision-Part-1/slides/ep03
// Run:    node --env-file=.env.local scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs Revision-Part-1:3
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  num: 3,
  series: 'Revision-Part-1',
  file: 'Episode 3 - Linear Equations',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode three. Linear equations.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `For two episodes we have been changing the shape of expressions. Today we start solving.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `First, my homework.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Factorise fully. Two x squared, plus ten x, plus twelve.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three terms. So it is a trinomial.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And what comes before that?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The common factor. Two goes into two, into ten, and into twelve. So I take out a two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two x squared divided by two is x squared. Ten x divided by two is five x. And twelve divided by two is six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So, two, bracket, x squared plus five x plus six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the bracket. Two numbers that multiply to six, and add to five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two and three. Two times three is six, and two plus three is five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So it is two, times x plus two, times x plus three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And taking that two out first is why the bracket was easy to see. That is the whole reason common factor goes first.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So what is different about today?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Until now, x could be anything. An expression is only a rule. Today there is an equals sign, and that changes everything.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `An equation says two things are the same. And usually only one value of x makes that true. Your job is to find it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And linear means?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Linear means every x is to the power of one. No x squared. No x cubed. No x underneath a square root.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And why does that matter?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Because a linear equation has exactly one answer. One x, one solution. When the x is squared, next episode, there are two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is the only idea you need today. Think of the equals sign as the middle of a balance scale.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Whatever you do to one side, you must do to the other. Otherwise it stops balancing, and the equation stops being true.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I can add anything I like, as long as I add it to both sides.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Add, subtract, multiply, divide. Both sides, every time. That is the entire topic.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the goal never changes. Get x on its own, on one side.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `First one. Three, bracket, two x minus five, equals four x plus seven.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `There are brackets on the left, and there is an x on both sides.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Then that tells you the order. Brackets first. Multiply the three into the bracket.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three times two x is six x. And three times negative five is negative fifteen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So six x minus fifteen equals four x plus seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now get the x terms onto one side. Take four x off both sides.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Six x minus four x is two x. And on the right, four x minus four x leaves nothing.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So two x minus fifteen equals seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the numbers onto the other side. Add fifteen to both sides.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative fifteen plus fifteen is nothing. And seven plus fifteen is twenty two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So two x equals twenty two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And two x means two times x. So to undo a times, you divide. Divide both sides by two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Twenty two divided by two is eleven. So x is eleven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now check it. Put eleven back into the question you were given, not into your own working.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The left side. Two times eleven is twenty two, minus five is seventeen. Three times seventeen is fifty one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The right side. Four times eleven is forty four, plus seven is fifty one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Fifty one and fifty one. It balances.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That check takes ten seconds, and it is worth every one of them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Second one, and this is a real matric question. November two thousand and eighteen, question two point two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You are given a sequence. Its enth term is negative seven en, plus forty two. Which term is equal to negative one hundred and forty?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I put negative one hundred and forty where the enth term is.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Exactly. And the moment you do that, it stops being a sequences question and becomes a linear equation in en.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative seven en, plus forty two, equals negative one hundred and forty.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Numbers to the right. Take forty two off both sides.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `On the right that gives negative one hundred and forty, minus forty two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Careful here. You are already below zero, and you are going further down, not back up.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So it is negative one hundred and eighty two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative seven en equals negative one hundred and eighty two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now divide both sides by negative seven.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `A negative divided by a negative is a positive. So it is one hundred and eighty two, divided by seven.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Seven into eighteen goes twice, and there is a remainder of four. Bring the two down, and that makes forty two. Seven into forty two goes six times.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So en is twenty six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The twenty sixth term. Three marks, and every one of them is linear work.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `But that came out of a sequences question.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It did, and that is the point. Nobody asks you to solve a linear equation on its own in matric. They hide one inside almost every question, and then they mark it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Third one. And this is where the marks actually go. Fractions.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `x divided by two, plus, bracket x plus three, divided by three, equals six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `I do not like fractions.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Nobody does. So get rid of them. Multiply every term by a number that both bottoms divide into.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two and three both go into six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Six it is. Now multiply each term by six. The quick way is to divide the six by the bottom of that term first, and then multiply by what is on top. And listen to the word every.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Six times x divided by two. Six divided by two is three, so that gives three x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Six times, bracket x plus three, divided by three. Six divided by three is two, so that gives two, bracket x plus three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So, three x, plus two bracket x plus three, equals six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Stop there. Look at the right hand side.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `I left the six alone.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You did. And that is the mistake almost everybody makes. The six is a term as well. It has to be multiplied like all the others.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `But finish it your way first, so you can see what it costs you.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two times x is two x, and two times three is six. So three x, plus two x, plus six, equals six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Five x plus six equals six. So five x is nothing. And if five times something is nothing, that something has to be zero. So x is zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now test it. Put zero into the question.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Zero divided by two is nothing. And zero plus three, divided by three, is one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That gives one. But the question says six. So I am wrong.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You are. Go back, and multiply the six as well.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Six times six is thirty six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So three x, plus two bracket x plus three, equals thirty six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now open the bracket.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two times x is two x. Two times three is six. So three x plus two x plus six equals thirty six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Five x plus six equals thirty six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Take six off both sides, then divide by five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Five x equals thirty. And thirty divided by five is six. So x is six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test it again.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Six divided by two is three. And six plus three, divided by three, is three. Three plus three is six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Six. It matches.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One last thing, and it is a rule people lose marks on for the whole of Grade twelve. Inequalities.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `An inequality is solved exactly like an equation, with one extra rule at the end.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Negative three x, plus five, is greater than fourteen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Take five off both sides. Negative three x is greater than nine.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now divide both sides by negative three. And here is the rule. When you multiply or divide by a negative, the sign turns around.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x is less than negative three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `But why does it turn around?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test it and you will see it for yourself. Try x equals negative four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative three times negative four is twelve. Twelve plus five is seventeen. And seventeen is greater than fourteen. So it works.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now try zero, which is above negative three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative three times zero is nothing. Nothing plus five is five. And five is not greater than fourteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the answers really are the numbers below negative three. The sign had to turn around, or you would have had the whole thing backwards.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Whatever you do, do it to both sides, and to every term.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. Fractions. Multiply every term by the bottom numbers, including the term that looks like it does not need it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. Dividing by a negative. In an equation it only changes the sign of your answer. In an inequality it turns the sign around as well.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. The order never changes. Brackets, then the fractions away, then the x terms to one side, then the numbers to the other, then divide.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. Always test your answer in the original question. A linear equation has one answer, so if it does not fit, it is wrong.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question. Bracket x minus one, divided by four, plus x divided by two, equals five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `I will give you the answer next time.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And what is next time?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Next time the x is squared. And a squared x gives you two answers, not one. Episode four. Quadratic equations.` },
