// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  num: 4,
  series: 'Revision-Part-1',
  file: 'Episode 4 - Quadratic Equations',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode four. Quadratic equations.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Before we start today, something has changed. From this episode, there is somebody new joining us.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Her name is Tyla, and she is going to be sitting where you are sitting. Asking the things you would ask if you could put up your hand.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Hello everyone. I am Tyla.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Welcome, Tyla. Now, fair warning. I am going to make you do the work.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is why I am here. And I already have homework, apparently.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You do. From last time. Solve, bracket x minus one, divided by four, plus x divided by two, equals five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `There are fractions, so I get rid of them first. The bottoms are four and two, and four is the smallest number they both go into.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Good. So multiply every term by four. And remember what caught us last time.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The five on the other side. It is a term too, so it gets multiplied as well.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Four times, bracket x minus one, divided by four. Four divided by four is one, so that leaves x minus one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Four times x divided by two. Four divided by two is two, so that gives two x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And four times five is twenty.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x minus one, plus two x, equals twenty.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Collect the x terms.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x plus two x is three x. So three x minus one equals twenty.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Add one to both sides. Three x equals twenty one. So x is seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And check it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Seven minus one is six, divided by four is one comma five. And seven divided by two is three comma five. One comma five plus three comma five is five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Five. Which is what the question said. Well done.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now. Today the x is squared. And that changes one thing, but it changes it completely.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `What changes?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The number of answers. A linear equation has one. A quadratic has two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Why two?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Think about x squared equals nine. What is x?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three works. But so does negative three, because negative three times negative three is also positive nine.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So there are two numbers that fit.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two numbers fit. And in the exam, if you write down only one of them, you lose a mark, even though what you wrote was true.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A quadratic is any equation where the highest power of x is two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And before you do anything else, you make one side zero. Everything on the left, nothing but zero on the right.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Why does it have to be zero?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Because of the rule from episode two. If two things multiply and give zero, one of them has to be zero. That only works against zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So if it equals six, I cannot split it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You cannot. Two things multiplying to give six could be two and three, or one and six, or anything. Zero is the only number that traps them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `First one. x squared plus x, equals twelve.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `It does not equal zero yet. So I take the twelve across.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x squared plus x minus twelve equals zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now factorise. Two numbers that multiply to negative twelve and add to positive one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative twelve, so one of them is negative. Four and three make twelve. And four minus three is one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So positive four and negative three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Write the brackets, and check your middle term.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Bracket x plus four, bracket x minus three. Middle term is negative three x plus four x, which is plus x. That matches.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now split it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x plus four equals zero, or x minus three equals zero. So x is negative four, or x is three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Both. Always both.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the second one, and this is a real matric question. November two thousand and sixteen, question one point one point two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x. x squared minus six x plus two equals zero. Three marks. And it says leave your answer correct to two decimal places.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two numbers that multiply to two and add to negative six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Try it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two is only one times two. And one plus two is three, not six. It does not factorise.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It does not. And that is not you failing. Most quadratics do not factorise. That is exactly why there is a formula.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And there is a clue in the question. When it tells you to round off, it is telling you the answer is not a whole number. So do not waste four minutes hunting for factors.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is useful.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The formula is on your formula sheet. x equals negative b, plus or minus the square root of b squared minus four a c, all of that divided by two a.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And what are a, b and c?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `a is the number in front of x squared. b is the number in front of x. c is the number on its own. Read them off with their signs.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So a is one, b is negative six, and c is two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Careful with b. Write it in brackets when you substitute, or the minus will get lost.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative, bracket negative six. Two negatives make a positive, so the top starts with six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Then plus or minus the square root of, bracket negative six, squared, minus four times one times two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative six squared is positive thirty six. And four times one times two is eight.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Thirty six minus eight is twenty eight.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the bottom?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two times a. a is one, so the bottom is two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x is six, plus or minus the square root of twenty eight, divided by two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The square root of twenty eight is five comma two nine, roughly. Now do the plus and the minus separately.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Six plus five comma two nine is eleven comma two nine, divided by two, is five comma six five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And six minus five comma two nine is zero comma seven one, divided by two, is zero comma three five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So x is five comma six five, or zero comma three five. Two decimal places, exactly as the question asked.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And one warning. Do not round the square root of twenty eight early and then work with it. Keep it in your calculator to the end, and round only the final answer.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Otherwise the last decimal comes out wrong.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that costs you the accuracy mark.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Third one. And this is where the marks disappear.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve. x squared equals four x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `There is an x squared and an x. I can divide both sides by x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Do it, and let us see what you get.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x squared divided by x is x. And four x divided by x is four. So x equals four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One answer. But I told you a quadratic has two. So where is the other one?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `I do not know. It just disappeared.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It did. And you deleted it yourself. Try zero in the original. Zero squared, and four times zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Zero squared is zero. And four times zero is zero. Zero equals zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So zero works too. And I lost it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You lost it the moment you divided by x. Because x can be zero here, and you are never allowed to divide by zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So dividing by x threw away the answer x equals zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Every time. Never divide an equation by the letter you are solving for. Make one side zero and take the letter out as a common factor instead.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So, x squared minus four x equals zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Both terms have an x. So x, bracket x minus four, equals zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Then x equals zero, or x minus four equals zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x equals zero, or x equals four. Both of them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Both of them. And in the exam that is a mark you would simply have handed back.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Make one side zero before you do anything. The zero rule is the only reason any of this works.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. Never divide by x. Take it out as a common factor, and you keep both answers.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. If it tells you to round off, it does not factorise. Go straight to the formula, and round only at the end.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. A quadratic has two answers. Write both, every time, even when one of them is zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. Try to factorise first. If it will not factorise in about thirty seconds, use the formula. It always works.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question. Solve for x. Two x squared minus five x minus three equals zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And I will do it with you.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Next time we take two equations at once. Episode five. Simultaneous equations.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:   0, title: `REVISION 1 • EPISODE 4 • QUADRATIC EQUATIONS`, onScreen: `EPISODE 4 — QUADRATIC EQUATIONS` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:   2, title: `MEET TYLA`, onScreen: `MICHAEL — your maths expert · TYLA — joining from today — she asks what you would ask` },
    { n:  3, afterSeg:   3, title: `WELCOME, TYLA`, onScreen: `now, back to work` },
    { n:  4, afterSeg:   6, title: `YOUR HOMEWORK`, onScreen: `(x − 1)/(4) + (x)/(2) = 5 · solve for x` },
    { n:  5, afterSeg:   7, title: `FRACTIONS FIRST`, onScreen: `multiply every term by 4` },
    { n:  6, afterSeg:  10, title: `AND THE 5 IS A TERM TOO`, onScreen: `4 × 5 = 20` },
    { n:  7, afterSeg:  13, title: `NO FRACTIONS LEFT`, onScreen: `x − 1 + 2x = 20` },
    { n:  8, afterSeg:  16, title: `COLLECT, THEN SOLVE`, onScreen: `x = 7 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n:  9, afterSeg:  18, title: `CHECK IT IN THE ORIGINAL`, onScreen: `1,5 + 3,5 = 5 ✓ ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg:  20, title: `TODAY THE X IS SQUARED`, onScreen: `and that changes the number of answers` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg:  22, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `LINEAR one answer · QUADRATIC two answers` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg:  24, title: `WHY TWO?`, onScreen: `x² = 9 · what is x?` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg:  26, title: `BOTH OF THESE ARE TRUE`, onScreen: `x = 3 or x = −3 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg:  28, title: `WRITE BOTH, EVERY TIME`, onScreen: `One answer on its own loses a mark — even though what you wrote was true.` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg:  29, title: `A QUADRATIC`, onScreen: `x² somewhere, and nothing higher` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg:  30, title: `FIRST, MAKE ONE SIDE ZERO`, onScreen: `everything on the left, nothing but 0 on the right` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg:  32, title: `WHY IT HAS TO BE ZERO`, onScreen: `that only works against zero — nothing else traps them` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg:  34, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `= 0 one bracket must be 0 · = 6 could be 2 and 3, or 1 and 6, or anything` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg:  35, title: `EXAMPLE 1`, onScreen: `x² + x = 12 · solve for x` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg:  37, title: `MAKE ONE SIDE ZERO`, onScreen: `x² + x − 12 = 0` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg:  40, title: `−12 MEANS ONE OF THEM IS NEGATIVE`, onScreen: `so +4 and −3` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg:  42, title: `CHECK THE MIDDLE TERM`, onScreen: `= +x ✓ it matches` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg:  44, title: `SPLIT IT`, onScreen: `x = −4 or x = 3 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg:  46, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • NOVEMBER 2016`, onScreen: `NSC Mathematics P1 · November 2016 · Question 1.1.2 · 3 marks` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg:  47, title: `QUESTION 1.1.2`, onScreen: `x² − 6x + 2 = 0 · Solve for x, correct to TWO decimal places.` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg:  50, title: `TRY TO FACTORISE FIRST`, onScreen: `red · it does not factorise ✗` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg:  51, title: `THAT IS NOT YOU FAILING`, onScreen: `Most quadratics do not factorise. That is exactly why there is a formula.` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg:  52, title: `“CORRECT TO TWO DECIMAL PLACES” IS A CLUE`, onScreen: `if it asks you to round, it does not factorise — go straight to the formula` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg:  54, title: `ON YOUR FORMULA SHEET`, onScreen: `x = (−b ± √b² − 4ac)/(2a)` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg:  56, title: `READ THEM OFF WITH THEIR SIGNS`, onScreen: `a — the number in front of x² · b — the number in front of x · c — the number on its own` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg:  57, title: `SO FOR THIS ONE`, onScreen: `a = 1 b = −6 c = 2` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg:  59, title: `BRACKET THE B OR THE MINUS GETS LOST`, onScreen: `the top starts with 6` },
    { n: 33, afterSeg:  61, title: `NOW UNDER THE ROOT`, onScreen: `36 − 8 = 28` },
    { n: 34, afterSeg:  64, title: `AND THE BOTTOM IS 2A`, onScreen: `x = (6 ± √28)/(2)` },
    { n: 35, afterSeg:  67, title: `TAKE THE PLUS VS TAKE THE MINUS`, onScreen: `x = 5,65 or x = 0,35` },
    { n: 36, afterSeg:  70, title: `ROUND ONLY AT THE END`, onScreen: `rounding early costs you the accuracy mark` },
    { n: 37, afterSeg:  74, title: `EXAMPLE 3`, onScreen: `x² = 4x · where the marks disappear` },
    { n: 38, afterSeg:  75, title: `TYLA DIVIDES BOTH SIDES BY X`, onScreen: `red · x = 4` },
    { n: 39, afterSeg:  78, title: `ONE ANSWER — BUT A QUADRATIC HAS TWO`, onScreen: `red · so where is the other one? ✗` },
    { n: 40, afterSeg:  81, title: `TEST X = 0 IN THE ORIGINAL`, onScreen: `0 = 0 ✓ so 0 works too` },
    { n: 41, afterSeg:  83, title: `SHE DELETED IT HERSELF`, onScreen: `x can be 0 here — and you may never divide by 0` },
    { n: 42, afterSeg:  85, title: `NEVER DIVIDE BY THE LETTER`, onScreen: `x = 0 is the one that disappears — take x out as a common factor instead` },
    { n: 43, afterSeg:  86, title: `DO IT PROPERLY — MAKE ONE SIDE ZERO`, onScreen: `x² − 4x = 0` },
    { n: 44, afterSeg:  87, title: `BOTH TERMS HAVE AN X`, onScreen: `x(x − 4) = 0` },
    { n: 45, afterSeg:  89, title: `NOW SPLIT IT`, onScreen: `x = 0 or x = 4 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 46, afterSeg:  90, title: `A MARK YOU HANDED BACK`, onScreen: `The working was right. The answer x = 0 was simply thrown away before it could be found.` },
    { n: 47, afterSeg:  91, title: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS`, onScreen: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS` },
    { n: 48, afterSeg:  92, title: `POINT 1 • MAKE ONE SIDE ZERO FIRST`, onScreen: `Make one side zero first — the zero rule is the only reason any of this works` },
    { n: 49, afterSeg:  93, title: `POINT 2 • NEVER DIVIDE BY X`, onScreen: `Never divide by x — take it out as a common factor and you keep both answers` },
    { n: 50, afterSeg:  94, title: `POINT 3 • TOLD TO ROUND? IT DOES NOT FACTORISE`, onScreen: `Told to round? It does not factorise — go straight to the formula — and round only at the end` },
    { n: 51, afterSeg:  95, title: `REMEMBER`, onScreen: `REMEMBER` },
    { n: 52, afterSeg:  96, title: `POINT 1 • A QUADRATIC HAS TWO ANSWERS`, onScreen: `A quadratic has two answers — write both, every time — even when one of them is zero` },
    { n: 53, afterSeg:  97, title: `POINT 2 • FACTORISE FIRST, FORMULA SECOND`, onScreen: `Factorise first, formula second — if it will not factorise in about 30 seconds, use the formula — it always works` },
    { n: 54, afterSeg:  98, title: `YOUR TURN`, onScreen: `2x² − 5x − 3 = 0 · solve for x` },
    { n: 55, afterSeg:  99, title: `PAUSE AND TRY`, onScreen: `PAUSE AND TRY` },
    { n: 56, afterSeg: 101, title: `NEXT • SIMULTANEOUS EQUATIONS`, onScreen: `NEXT — EPISODE 5: SIMULTANEOUS EQUATIONS` },
  ],
},
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// REVISION PART 1 — EPISODE 5: Equations — Simultaneous
// Paste into the EPISODES array in scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs,
// next to the existing series: 'Revision-Part-1' episode 1-4 objects.
// Slides: unzip into podcasts/Working-It-Out/Revision-Part-1/slides/ep05
// Run:    node --env-file=.env.local scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs Revision-Part-1:5
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  num: 5,
  series: 'Revision-Part-1',
  file: 'Episode 5 - Simultaneous Equations',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode five. Simultaneous equations.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two episodes of solving for x. Today there are two letters, and two equations.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Homework first.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x. Two x squared, minus five x, minus three, equals zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `It already equals zero, so I go straight to factorising. But there is a two in front of the x squared.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So you try an arrangement and check the middle term. Same as episode two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two times negative three is negative six. So I need something that gives me negative five x in the middle.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `I will try, bracket two x plus one, bracket x minus three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Check the middle.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two x times negative three is negative six x. And one times x is x. Negative six x plus x is negative five x. That matches.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now split it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two x plus one equals zero, or x minus three equals zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The second one gives x equals three. And the first one gives two x equals negative one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So x is?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative one divided by two. Negative a half.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Negative a half, or three. And a fraction is a perfectly good answer. Do not throw it away because it looks untidy.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now. Everything so far has had one letter in it. Today we get two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two letters at once. That sounds worse.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It is not, and here is why. Look at x plus y equals ten. Can you tell me what x is?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `No. x could be one and y could be nine. Or x could be four and y could be six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Endless answers. One equation with two letters can never pin them down.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I need something else to narrow it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You need a second equation. And that is the whole idea. Two unknowns need two equations.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The method has four steps, and they never change.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. Take the simpler equation, the one without squares, and make one letter the subject.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. Substitute that into the other equation. Now there is only one letter left.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three. Solve it, the way you already know how.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Four. Go back and find the partner value for each answer.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And step four is the one people forget?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Step four is where half the marks live. Hold that thought.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `First one. x plus y equals seven. And two x minus y equals five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Both are simple, so I can pick either. I will make y the subject of the first one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `y equals seven minus x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now substitute that into the second equation, in the place where y is. And use a bracket.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two x minus, bracket, seven minus x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Why the bracket?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Because the minus in front has to hit both terms, not just the seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That bracket is the whole question. Open it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Minus seven, and minus a negative x, which is plus x. So two x minus seven plus x equals five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three x minus seven equals five. Three x equals twelve. So x is four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And step four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `y equals seven minus four. So y is three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Check both equations, not just one. Four plus three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Seven. And two times four is eight, minus three is five. Both of them work.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the real one. November two thousand and seventeen, question one point two. Six marks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x and y at the same time. Three x minus y equals four. And x squared, plus two x y, minus y squared, equals negative two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The second one has squares in it. So I use the first one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Always. Make a letter the subject of the linear equation, never the quadratic one. Which letter is easiest here?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `y, because it has nothing in front of it. Three x minus four equals y.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So y equals three x minus four. Now substitute. And bracket it everywhere it goes.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x squared, plus two x times bracket three x minus four, minus bracket three x minus four, squared. All equal to negative two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Take the middle piece first.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two x times three x is six x squared. And two x times negative four is negative eight x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the last piece. Careful, it is a bracket squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I write it out twice. Bracket three x minus four, times bracket three x minus four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three x times three x is nine x squared. Then negative twelve x, and negative twelve x again. And negative four times negative four is positive sixteen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So that bracket is nine x squared, minus twenty four x, plus sixteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And there is a minus sign sitting in front of the whole thing.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So every sign inside flips. Negative nine x squared, plus twenty four x, minus sixteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is the step that costs people the question. Now put all three pieces together.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x squared, plus six x squared, minus nine x squared, is negative two x squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative eight x plus twenty four x is sixteen x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And then minus sixteen, equals negative two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Make one side zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative two x squared, plus sixteen x, minus sixteen, plus two, equals zero. So minus fourteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now, before you factorise. Every term there divides by negative two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I divide the whole equation by negative two. That gives x squared, minus eight x, plus seven, equals zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Much friendlier. And dividing an equation through by a number is always allowed, as long as you divide every term.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two numbers that multiply to seven and add to negative eight. Negative one and negative seven.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So bracket x minus one, bracket x minus seven, equals zero. x is one, or x is seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Four marks so far. Now step four, for two marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `y equals three x minus four. So for x equals one, y is three minus four, which is negative one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And for x equals seven, y is twenty one minus four, which is seventeen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So write your answer as pairs. x is one with y is negative one. And x is seven with y is seventeen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the trap, and it is a cruel one. Watch what happens if you mix the pairs up.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Suppose you wrote x equals one with y equals seventeen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `They are both answers I found, so surely that is fine.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test it. Three times one, minus seventeen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three minus seventeen is negative fourteen. And the equation says four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So that pair is wrong, even though both numbers are right.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Each x belongs to one y. They are not interchangeable. Keep them together on the page as you find them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And there is one more way to lose marks here, which is simply to stop early.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Stop after finding x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You have four of the six marks and the question is only half answered. It says solve for x and y.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Make a letter the subject of the linear equation, never the one with squares in it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. Bracket everything you substitute, and remember that a minus in front flips every sign inside.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. Finish. Find the partner for every answer, and keep the pairs together.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. Two unknowns need two equations. One equation on its own can never pin them down.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. Substitute back into the simple equation, not the hard one. It is less work and it cannot give you extra answers.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question. Solve for x and y at the same time. y equals x plus two. And x squared plus y squared equals ten.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And remember to give your answers in pairs.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Next time, the sign that turns around. Episode six. Inequalities.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:   0, title: `REVISION 1 • EPISODE 5 • SIMULTANEOUS EQUATIONS`, onScreen: `EPISODE 5 — SIMULTANEOUS EQUATIONS` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:   1, title: `TWO LETTERS, AND TWO EQUATIONS`, onScreen: `two episodes of solving for one — today there are two` },
    { n:  3, afterSeg:   3, title: `YOUR HOMEWORK`, onScreen: `2x² − 5x − 3 = 0 · solve for x` },
    { n:  4, afterSeg:   4, title: `ALREADY EQUALS ZERO`, onScreen: `but there is a 2 in front of the x²` },
    { n:  5, afterSeg:   8, title: `TRY AN ARRANGEMENT`, onScreen: `now check the middle term` },
    { n:  6, afterSeg:  10, title: `CHECK THE MIDDLE`, onScreen: `−6x + x = −5x ✓` },
    { n:  7, afterSeg:  13, title: `SPLIT IT`, onScreen: `x = (−1)/(2)` },
    { n:  8, afterSeg:  15, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `x = − (1)/(2) or x = 3 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n:  9, afterSeg:  18, title: `ONE EQUATION, TWO LETTERS`, onScreen: `x + y = 10 · so what is x?` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg:  19, title: `ALL OF THESE FIT`, onScreen: `endless answers — nothing is pinned down` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg:  22, title: `TWO UNKNOWNS NEED TWO EQUATIONS`, onScreen: `the second one is what narrows it to a single pair` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg:  23, title: `THE FOUR STEPS`, onScreen: `THE FOUR STEPS` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg:  27, title: `AND THEY NEVER CHANGE`, onScreen: `1 — make a letter the subject of the simple equation · 2 — substitute it into the other one · 3 — solve, the way you already know · 4 — go back for the partner value` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg:  29, title: `STEP FOUR IS WHERE HALF THE MARKS LIVE`, onScreen: `and it is the one people forget` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg:  30, title: `EXAMPLE 1`, onScreen: `x + y = 7 and 2x − y = 5 · solve for x and y` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg:  32, title: `STEP 1 — MAKE Y THE SUBJECT`, onScreen: `y = 7 − x` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg:  34, title: `STEP 2 — SUBSTITUTE, IN A BRACKET`, onScreen: `2x − (7 − x) = 5` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg:  36, title: `WHY THE BRACKET`, onScreen: `that bracket is the whole question` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg:  38, title: `OPEN IT — EVERY SIGN INSIDE FLIPS`, onScreen: `2x − 7 + x = 5` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg:  39, title: `STEP 3 — SOLVE`, onScreen: `x = 4` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg:  41, title: `STEP 4 — THE PARTNER`, onScreen: `x = 4 and y = 3 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg:  43, title: `CHECK THE FIRST VS CHECK THE SECOND`, onScreen: `(4 ; 3)` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg:  44, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • NOVEMBER 2017`, onScreen: `NSC Mathematics P1 · November 2017 · Question 1.2 · 6 marks` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg:  45, title: `QUESTION 1.2`, onScreen: `3x − y = 4 and x² + 2xy − y² = −2 · Solve for x and y simultaneously.` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg:  48, title: `STEP 1 — USE THE LINEAR ONE`, onScreen: `y = 3x − 4` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg:  50, title: `STEP 2 — SUBSTITUTE, BRACKETS EVERYWHERE`, onScreen: `x² + 2x(3x − 4) − (3x − 4)² = −2` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg:  52, title: `THE MIDDLE PIECE`, onScreen: `2x × (−4) = −8x` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg:  55, title: `THE SQUARED BRACKET — WRITE IT OUT TWICE`, onScreen: `9x² − 24x + 16` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg:  57, title: `BUT THERE IS A MINUS IN FRONT OF IT`, onScreen: `−9x² + 24x − 16` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg:  58, title: `THIS IS THE STEP THAT COSTS THE QUESTION`, onScreen: `A minus in front of a bracket changes every sign inside it. All three.` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg:  61, title: `PUT THE THREE PIECES TOGETHER`, onScreen: `−2x² + 16x − 16 = −2` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg:  63, title: `MAKE ONE SIDE ZERO`, onScreen: `−2x² + 16x − 14 = 0` },
    { n: 33, afterSeg:  65, title: `EVERY TERM DIVIDES BY −2`, onScreen: `x² − 8x + 7 = 0` },
    { n: 34, afterSeg:  66, title: `DIVIDING THROUGH IS ALWAYS ALLOWED`, onScreen: `it makes the factorising far easier` },
    { n: 35, afterSeg:  68, title: `FACTORISE`, onScreen: `x = 1 or x = 7` },
    { n: 36, afterSeg:  69, title: `FOUR MARKS — AND THE QUESTION IS HALF ANSWERED`, onScreen: `step 4 is worth the other two` },
    { n: 37, afterSeg:  71, title: `STEP 4 — THE PARTNER FOR EACH`, onScreen: `two answers, two pairs` },
    { n: 38, afterSeg:  72, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `(1 ; −1) or (7 ; 17) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 39, afterSeg:  73, title: `THE TRAP — MIXING THE PAIRS`, onScreen: `THE TRAP — MIXING THE PAIRS` },
    { n: 40, afterSeg:  74, title: `SUPPOSE YOU WROTE THIS`, onScreen: `red · x = 1 with y = 17` },
    { n: 41, afterSeg:  77, title: `TEST IT IN THE LINEAR EQUATION`, onScreen: `red · = −14, not 4 ✗` },
    { n: 42, afterSeg:  79, title: `EACH x BELONGS TO ONE y`, onScreen: `keep them together on the page as you find them` },
    { n: 43, afterSeg:  81, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `STOP AFTER x 4 out of 6 · FINISH THE PAIRS 6 out of 6` },
    { n: 44, afterSeg:  83, title: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS`, onScreen: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS` },
    { n: 45, afterSeg:  84, title: `POINT 1 • USE THE LINEAR EQUATION`, onScreen: `Use the linear equation — never make a letter the subject of the one with squares in it` },
    { n: 46, afterSeg:  85, title: `POINT 2 • BRACKET EVERYTHING YOU SUBSTITUTE`, onScreen: `Bracket everything you substitute — a minus in front of a bracket flips every sign inside it` },
    { n: 47, afterSeg:  86, title: `POINT 3 • FINISH — FIND EVERY PARTNER`, onScreen: `Finish — find every partner — and keep each pair together` },
    { n: 48, afterSeg:  87, title: `REMEMBER`, onScreen: `REMEMBER` },
    { n: 49, afterSeg:  88, title: `POINT 1 • TWO UNKNOWNS NEED TWO EQUATIONS`, onScreen: `Two unknowns need two equations — one on its own can never pin them down` },
    { n: 50, afterSeg:  89, title: `POINT 2 • SUBSTITUTE BACK INTO THE SIMPLE ONE`, onScreen: `Substitute back into the simple one — less work, and it cannot hand you extra answers` },
