  ],
},
{
  num: 6,
  series: 'Revision-Part-1',
  file: 'Episode 6 - Quadratic Inequalities',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode six. Quadratic inequalities.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Everything so far has ended with an equals sign. Today the sign changes, and so does the shape of the answer.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Homework first, though.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Of course. Solve for x and y at the same time. y equals x plus two. And x squared plus y squared equals ten.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The first one is already linear, and y is already on its own. So I substitute it straight into the second one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Good. And bracket it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x squared, plus bracket x plus two, all squared, equals ten.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Bracket x plus two, squared, is x squared plus four x plus four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Careful there. It is not x squared plus four. The middle term is easy to lose.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x squared plus x squared plus four x plus four equals ten. That is two x squared plus four x plus four equals ten.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Make one side zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two x squared plus four x minus six equals zero. And every term divides by two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Which you should always check for. It makes the factorising far easier.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x squared plus two x minus three equals zero. Two numbers that multiply to negative three and add to two. Three and negative one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So bracket x plus three, bracket x minus one, equals zero. x is negative three, or x is one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And finish. Every x needs its partner.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `y equals x plus two. So negative three gives y equals negative one. And one gives y equals three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the pairs are, negative three with negative one, and one with three. Check the second equation quickly.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Nine plus one is ten. And one plus nine is ten. Both work.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now. Today the equals sign becomes an inequality sign.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Less than, or greater than.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that changes what the answer looks like. Not a number. A stretch of the number line.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `A stretch?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Think about it simply. x squared equals nine has two answers, three and negative three. Two points.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `But x squared less than nine is asking something wider. Which numbers, when you square them, land below nine?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two would. Two squared is four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And so would zero, and one, and one comma five, and negative two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So it is everything in between.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Everything between negative three and three. An unbroken stretch. That is why the answer is written as a range, not a list.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the two numbers at the edges of that stretch have a name. The critical values.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Where do they come from?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `From solving it as if it were an equation. The critical values are the fence posts. The inequality sign tells you which side of the fence to stand on.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the method has four steps, and step one never changes.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. Make one side zero. Two. Factorise. Three. Read off the critical values. Four. Choose the side, and write it properly.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example one, and it is straight out of a matric paper.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, November two thousand and twelve. Question one point one point three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x. x squared plus seven x minus eight, is less than zero. Four marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One side is already zero. So I go straight to factorising.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Exactly. And notice you factorise it the same way you always have. The inequality sign does not change that part at all.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two numbers that multiply to negative eight and add to seven. Eight and negative one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So bracket x plus eight, bracket x minus one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the critical values. Pretend for a second that it says equals zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Then x would be negative eight, or x would be one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Those are your two fence posts. Draw a number line and mark them.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative eight on the left, one on the right.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `They cut the line into three pieces. To the left of negative eight. Between the two. And to the right of one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The question asks where the expression is less than zero. So, where is it negative.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `How do I tell?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test one number from each piece. That is all. Pick zero, it sits between them.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Zero squared is zero, plus seven times zero is zero, minus eight. So negative eight.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Negative. And the question wants negative. So the middle piece is the answer.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And the outside pieces?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Try two. Four, plus fourteen, minus eight, is ten. Positive. So the outside is not what we want.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now write it. Negative eight is less than x, is less than one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One line, with x in the middle.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is exactly what the marking guideline wants. It also accepts x is an element of the interval from negative eight to one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So either notation is fine.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Either one. But whichever you choose, finish it. Stopping at the critical values gets you three of the four marks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And now the pattern that saves you time in the exam.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `When the x squared is positive, and the expression is less than zero, the answer is always the piece in between.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And greater than zero?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Always the two outside pieces. Never the middle.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Less than is in. Greater than is out.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is it. But test a number anyway. It takes five seconds and it cannot lie to you.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One more thing about the writing. When it is greater than, you have two separate pieces, so you use the word or.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x is less than negative eight, or x is greater than one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And you can never squash that into one line. There is no number that is both bigger than one and smaller than negative eight.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So writing one is less than x is less than negative eight would be nonsense.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It would, and learners write it every year.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example two. Same topic, harder question, and again from the real paper.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, November two thousand and fourteen. Question one point three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x. Bracket x minus one, bracket x minus two, is less than six. Four marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `It is already factorised. So the critical values are one and two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `No. And that is the single most expensive mistake in this whole topic.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Why not? It looks factorised.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Because it is not against zero. It is against six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Think back to episode four. Two things multiplying to give zero means one of them must be zero. That is a real rule.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And two things multiplying to give six?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Could be two and three. Could be one and six. Could be twelve and a half. Six traps nothing.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So the brackets are useless while the six is sitting there.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Useless. So expand, and start again properly.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x times x is x squared. x times negative two is negative two x. Negative one times x is negative x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And negative one times negative two is positive two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Collect the middle.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative two x minus x is negative three x. So x squared minus three x plus two, is less than six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now step one. Make one side zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Take the six across. Two minus six is negative four. So x squared minus three x minus four, is less than zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now factorise.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two numbers that multiply to negative four and add to negative three. Negative four and one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So bracket x plus one, bracket x minus four, is less than zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the critical values.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative one and four. Which are nothing like one and two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Nothing like them. That is what the expanding was for.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Less than zero, so the middle piece. Test zero. Nothing, minus nothing, minus four, is negative four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative. So negative one is less than x, is less than four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is the memorandum answer, word for word.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example three. And this time, Tyla, just answer it the way it feels natural.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x. x squared is greater than nine.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is easy. The square root of nine is three. So x is greater than three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test a number. Try negative five.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative five squared is twenty five. Twenty five is greater than nine.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So negative five works. But negative five is not greater than three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So your answer threw away half the solutions. And this is the trap, because square rooting feels like the right move.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So what should I have done?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The same four steps. Never square root an inequality. Make one side zero first.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x squared minus nine, is greater than zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is a difference of squares, from episode two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Bracket x minus three, bracket x plus three, is greater than zero. So the critical values are three and negative three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Greater than zero. So?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Outside. x is less than negative three, or x is greater than three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And now negative five is included, exactly as it should be. Test it again.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative five is less than negative three. Yes. It fits.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One question, two pieces, and the word or holding them together.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Make one side zero before you touch the brackets. Factors only mean something against zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. The critical values are not the answer. They are the fence posts. You still have to say which side.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. Never square root an inequality. You lose the negative half every time.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. Less than zero is the piece in between. Greater than zero is the two outside pieces, joined by the word or.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. When you are unsure, test a number. One substitution settles it, and it costs you five seconds.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question. Solve for x. x squared minus x minus six, is greater than or equal to zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And watch that sign. Greater than or equal to. The circles close, and the fence posts are included.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Next time, the powers. Episode seven. Exponents.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:   0, title: `REVISION 1 • EPISODE 6 • QUADRATIC INEQUALITIES`, onScreen: `EPISODE 6 — QUADRATIC INEQUALITIES` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:   1, title: `TODAY THE EQUALS SIGN CHANGES`, onScreen: `and so does the shape of the answer` },
    { n:  3, afterSeg:   3, title: `YOUR HOMEWORK`, onScreen: `y = x + 2 and x² + y² = 10 · solve for x and y` },
    { n:  4, afterSeg:   4, title: `SUBSTITUTE THE LINEAR ONE`, onScreen: `so x + 2 goes in for y` },
    { n:  5, afterSeg:   5, title: `AND BRACKET IT`, onScreen: `x² + (x + 2)² = 10` },
    { n:  6, afterSeg:   7, title: `SQUARE THE BRACKET IN FULL`, onScreen: `x² + 4x + 4` },
    { n:  7, afterSeg:   8, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `(x + 2)² x² + 4x + 4 · not x² + 4 the middle term is the one that gets lost` },
    { n:  8, afterSeg:   9, title: `COLLECT`, onScreen: `2x² + 4x + 4 = 10` },
    { n:  9, afterSeg:  10, title: `MAKE ONE SIDE ZERO`, onScreen: `2x² + 4x − 6 = 0` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg:  11, title: `EVERY TERM DIVIDES BY 2`, onScreen: `x² + 2x − 3 = 0` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg:  12, title: `ALWAYS CHECK FOR A COMMON FACTOR`, onScreen: `as long as you divide every term` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg:  13, title: `TWO NUMBERS`, onScreen: `3 and −1` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg:  14, title: `FACTORISE, THEN SPLIT`, onScreen: `x = −3 or x = 1` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg:  15, title: `EVERY X NEEDS ITS PARTNER`, onScreen: `the question says solve for x and y` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg:  16, title: `FIND EACH PARTNER`, onScreen: `−3 → y = −1 · 1 → y = 3` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg:  17, title: `KEEP THE PAIRS TOGETHER`, onScreen: `(−3 ; −1) and (1 ; 3) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg:  18, title: `CHECK IN THE SECOND EQUATION`, onScreen: `both pairs work ✓ ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg:  19, title: `INEQUALITIES`, onScreen: `INEQUALITIES` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg:  20, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `= an equation · < > an inequality` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg:  21, title: `THE ANSWER IS NOT A NUMBER`, onScreen: `it is a stretch of the number line` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg:  23, title: `WHY X SQUARED EQUALS NINE HAS TWO ANSWERS`, onScreen: `x = 3 or x = −3 · two points` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg:  24, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `x² = 9 two points — 3 and −3 · x² < 9 which numbers land below nine?` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg:  25, title: `START TESTING NUMBERS`, onScreen: `2 · 2 squared is 4, and 4 is below 9` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg:  26, title: `AND SO WOULD ALL OF THESE`, onScreen: `0 · 1 · 1,5 · 2 · −2` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg:  28, title: `AN UNBROKEN STRETCH — SO WRITE IT AS A RANGE`, onScreen: `−3 < x < 3 (boxed)` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg:  29, title: `THE CRITICAL VALUES`, onScreen: `The two numbers at the edges of the stretch.` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg:  31, title: `THEY ARE FENCE POSTS`, onScreen: `the sign then tells you which side of them to stand on` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg:  32, title: `FOUR STEPS — AND STEP ONE NEVER CHANGES`, onScreen: `the same opening move on every one of these` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg:  33, title: `THE FOUR STEPS`, onScreen: `1 make one side zero · 2 factorise · 3 read off the critical values · 4 choose the side, and write it properly` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg:  34, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • NOVEMBER 2012`, onScreen: `NSC Mathematics P1 · November 2012 · Question 1.1.3 · 4 marks` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg:  36, title: `QUESTION 1.1.3`, onScreen: `x² + 7x − 8 < 0 · Solve for x.` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg:  37, title: `ONE SIDE IS ALREADY ZERO`, onScreen: `go straight to factorising` },
    { n: 33, afterSeg:  38, title: `THE SIGN CHANGES NOTHING HERE`, onScreen: `You factorise an inequality exactly the way you factorise an equation.` },
    { n: 34, afterSeg:  39, title: `TWO NUMBERS`, onScreen: `8 and −1` },
    { n: 35, afterSeg:  40, title: `SO IT FACTORISES AS`, onScreen: `(x + 8)(x − 1) < 0` },
    { n: 36, afterSeg:  41, title: `PRETEND IT SAYS EQUALS ZERO`, onScreen: `that is where the fence posts are` },
    { n: 37, afterSeg:  42, title: `THE CRITICAL VALUES`, onScreen: `x = −8 or x = 1` },
    { n: 38, afterSeg:  44, title: `MARK THEM ON A NUMBER LINE`, onScreen: `NUMBER LINE` },
    { n: 39, afterSeg:  45, title: `TWO POSTS, THREE PIECES`, onScreen: `to the left · in between · to the right` },
    { n: 40, afterSeg:  46, title: `LESS THAN ZERO MEANS: WHERE IS IT NEGATIVE?`, onScreen: `that is the only question the sign is asking` },
    { n: 41, afterSeg:  48, title: `TEST ONE NUMBER FROM EACH PIECE`, onScreen: `That is all it takes. Start with zero — it sits between them.` },
    { n: 42, afterSeg:  49, title: `TEST ZERO — IT SITS IN THE MIDDLE`, onScreen: `= −8 · negative ✓` },
    { n: 43, afterSeg:  50, title: `NEGATIVE IS WHAT THE QUESTION WANTS`, onScreen: `so the middle piece is the answer` },
    { n: 44, afterSeg:  52, title: `AND TEST TWO, ON THE OUTSIDE`, onScreen: `= 10 · positive ✗` },
    { n: 45, afterSeg:  53, title: `LESS THAN ZERO — TAKE THE MIDDLE`, onScreen: `−8 < x < 1 (boxed)` },
    { n: 46, afterSeg:  55, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `−8 < x < 1 one line, with x in the middle · x ∈ (−8 ; 1) interval notation — also accepted` },
    { n: 47, afterSeg:  57, title: `DO NOT STOP AT THE CRITICAL VALUES`, onScreen: `it is three of the four marks` },
    { n: 48, afterSeg:  58, title: `THE PATTERN`, onScreen: `THE PATTERN` },
    { n: 49, afterSeg:  59, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `< 0 the piece in between · > 0 the two outside pieces` },
    { n: 50, afterSeg:  61, title: `GREATER THAN ZERO IS NEVER THE MIDDLE`, onScreen: `it is always the two outer pieces` },
    { n: 51, afterSeg:  62, title: `LESS THAN IS IN. GREATER THAN IS OUT.`, onScreen: `when the x² is positive` },
    { n: 52, afterSeg:  63, title: `TEST A NUMBER ANYWAY`, onScreen: `Five seconds, and it cannot lie to you.` },
