  ],
},
{
  num: 7,
  series: 'Revision-Part-1',
  file: 'Episode 7 - Exponents',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode seven. Exponents.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The powers. And in Paper One they are almost always hiding inside a solve for x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Homework first.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x. x squared, minus x, minus six, is greater than or equal to zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One side is already zero, so I go straight to factorising.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two numbers that multiply to negative six and add to negative one. Negative three and two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So bracket x minus three, bracket x plus two, is greater than or equal to zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Critical values.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three and negative two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Greater than zero. So which side?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Outside. And the sign has an equal to in it, so the circles close.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Which means the critical values themselves are part of the answer.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x is less than or equal to negative two, or x is greater than or equal to three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test one. Try zero, from the middle. Nothing, minus nothing, minus six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative six. And we wanted greater than or equal to zero, so the middle is out. That checks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now. Exponents.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The laws.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Six of them, and they are on nobody’s formula sheet. You have to carry them in your head.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. When you multiply powers of the same base, you add the exponents.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. When you divide them, you subtract.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three. A power raised to another power, you multiply the exponents.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Four. Anything to the power of zero is one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Five. A negative exponent means one divided by that power.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Six. A fractional exponent is a root, and the bottom number is the root.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x to the power of one third is the cube root of x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Exactly. And then there is one more thing, which is not a law, but it is the whole game.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `If the bases are the same, and the two sides are equal, then the exponents are equal.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So two to the power of x, equals two to the power of five, means x is five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is how every one of these questions ends. Your only job is to get both sides onto the same base.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example one, straight from the paper.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, November two thousand and fourteen. Question one point one point three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x. Two to the power of, x plus two, plus two to the power of x, equals twenty. Three marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Can I add the exponents? Two to the power of, two x plus two?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test it. If x were two, that would be two to the power of six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Sixty four. And the question says twenty.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So no. You add exponents when the powers are multiplied. These two are added, and adding does nothing to exponents.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Then what do I do with them?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You take out a common factor. Exactly like taking x out of x squared plus x, back in episode two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `But first, split the awkward one. Two to the power of, x plus two, is two to the power of x, times two squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Because multiplying adds the exponents. So the x plus two comes back apart.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Right. So the left side is now two squared, times two to the power of x, plus two to the power of x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And both terms have two to the power of x in them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So take it out the front.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two to the power of x, bracket four plus one. Which is five, times two to the power of x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that line is a mark on its own. The marking guideline gives one just for the common factor.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Five times two to the power of x equals twenty. So two to the power of x is four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Same base now.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Four is two squared. So two to the power of x equals two squared. And x is two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Check it. Two to the power of four is sixteen. Two squared is four. Sixteen plus four is twenty. Correct.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example two. And this one is in the no calculator paper.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, November two thousand and fifteen. Question one point one point three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x. Two, times x to the power of negative five thirds, equals sixty four. Four marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The exponent is on the x this time. Not on a number.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Which turns the method around. Before, the letter was up in the exponent. Now the letter is the base.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So first, get the x on its own. That two in front is multiplying.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So divide both sides by two. x to the power of negative five thirds, equals thirty two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now, to undo a power, you raise both sides to the reciprocal of that power.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The reciprocal of negative five thirds is negative three fifths.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So x equals thirty two, to the power of negative three fifths.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That looks worse than what I started with.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It is not, because thirty two is a power of two. Thirty two is two to the power of five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And rewriting a number as a power is a marked step. The guideline gives you a mark just for spotting it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So it is two to the power of five, all raised to the power of negative three fifths.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A power raised to a power. Multiply the exponents.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Five times negative three fifths is negative three. So two to the power of negative three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And a negative exponent means one divided by the power.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One divided by two cubed. Which is one divided by eight.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One eighth. Or zero comma one two five. The guideline accepts either.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And no calculator anywhere in that.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `None. Which is exactly why they chose thirty two and sixty four. Learn the powers of two, up to two to the power of ten.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example three. This is the harder shape, and it carries five marks.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, November two thousand and twenty four. Question one point one point four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Solve for x. Two to the power of two x, minus two to the power of, x plus two, minus thirty two, equals zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three terms, and it equals zero. That has the shape of a quadratic.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It is one, in disguise. Split the middle term first.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two to the power of, x plus two, is two to the power of x, times four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And now the first term. Two to the power of two x is two to the power of x, all squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Because a power raised to a power multiplies. x times two is two x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So if you treat two to the power of x as one single thing, you have that thing squared, minus four of that thing, minus thirty two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Which factorises. Two numbers that multiply to negative thirty two and add to negative four. Negative eight and four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So write it out.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Bracket two to the power of x, minus eight. Bracket two to the power of x, plus four. Equals zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And split.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two to the power of x is eight. Or two to the power of x is negative four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Eight is two cubed, so x is three. And for the negative four, a negative exponent, so x is negative two?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Test that. Work out two to the power of negative two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One divided by two squared. Which is one divided by four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is positive. It is small, but it is positive.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is the rule you need. Two to the power of anything is always positive. Always.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A big exponent gives a big answer. A negative exponent gives a small answer. But never zero, and never negative.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So two to the power of x equals negative four has no solution at all.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `None. And you must write that down. The marking guideline gives a mark for the rejection.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So the answer is just x equals three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Check it. Two to the power of six is sixty four. Two to the power of five is thirty two. Sixty four, minus thirty two, minus thirty two, is zero. Correct.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Exponents only add when the powers are multiplied. Adding two powers does nothing to their exponents.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. Rewrite every number as a power of the same base. Thirty two is two to the power of five, and that is a mark.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. A power of a positive base is never negative. Reject it, and say so in writing.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. Take out the common power. It is the one with the smaller exponent that comes out.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. Learn the powers of two and three by heart. These questions are built on them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question. Solve for x. Three to the power of, x plus one, minus three to the power of x, equals fifty four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Take out the common power first. Everything follows from that.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Next time, the roots. Episode eight. Surds.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:   0, title: `REVISION 1 • EPISODE 7 • EXPONENTS`, onScreen: `EPISODE 7 — EXPONENTS` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:   1, title: `IN PAPER ONE THE POWERS HIDE INSIDE A SOLVE…`, onScreen: `never a chapter exercise — always question one` },
