},
{
  num: 1,
  series: 'Functions',
  file: 'Episode 1 - Revision',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Working It Out. Functions. Episode one. Revision.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A new series. And a change of shape, because from here on the answers are pictures.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Homework first, from the last series.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The roots of a quadratic equation are x equals three, plus or minus the square root of, negative k minus four, all divided by two. For which values of k are the roots real?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Real means the discriminant is greater than or equal to zero. And the discriminant is sitting under the root.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So negative k minus four, is greater than or equal to zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now solve it, and watch what happens.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative k is greater than or equal to four. And then I divide by negative one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Which turns the sign around. So k is less than or equal to negative four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Good. Test it. Take k equals negative five. Negative, of negative five, is five. Minus four is one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The square root of one is one. Real. It works.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now. Functions.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `We did straight lines and parabolas in Grade ten.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You did. And Grade twelve does not teach them again. It assumes them, and then builds inverses on top.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So this episode is the revision. Four shapes, and what the examiner asks about each one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the shapes live in questions four, five and six of Paper One. Not question one. Somewhere between forty and fifty marks of the paper.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Which four shapes?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The straight line. The parabola. The hyperbola. And the exponential.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You must know each one from its equation, before you draw anything.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A straight line has an x on its own. A parabola has an x squared. A hyperbola has the x underneath, on the bottom of a fraction.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And an exponential has the x up in the air, as the power.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So it is the position of the x that tells you the shape.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Every time. And once you know the shape, the examiner wants the same list of things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Intercepts. Asymptotes. Turning point. Domain. Range.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Can we do those words properly? Because I nod at them and I am not always sure.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `We can, and you should, because those words are the marks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `An intercept is where the graph crosses an axis. For the y intercept you make x zero. For the x intercept you make y zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So you set the other one to zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Always. An asymptote is a line the graph gets closer and closer to but never actually touches.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The domain is all the x values you are allowed to use. The range is all the y values that come out.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `In means domain, out means range.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is exactly it. And one more word, which the exam asks for by name.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A function is increasing if the y values climb as you move to the right, and decreasing if they fall.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And a parabola does both.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It does, so for a parabola you have to say where. One side climbs, the other falls, and the turning point is where it changes over.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now, there is one step before any of this, and it is the first line of working in nearly every one of these questions.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Get y on its own.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Rearrange it first.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Always. If the paper hands you three y minus six x plus nine equals zero, you can read nothing at all off that.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I divide the whole thing by three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And you get y equals two x minus three. Now the gradient and the y intercept are simply sitting there, waiting.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Standard form first, every time.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Every time. Now, three real exam questions, one shape at a time.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `First one, from the November two thousand and twelve paper, question four point one. Seven marks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Consider the function, f of x equals three, times two to the power of x, minus six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The x is the power. So it is exponential.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Right. Part one. Calculate the y intercept.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `y intercept, so I make x zero. Three times two to the power of zero, minus six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And anything to the power of zero?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Is one. So three times one is three. Minus six is negative three. The point is zero, negative three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part two. The x intercept.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Now I make y zero. So zero equals three, times two to the power of x, minus six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Take the six across. Three times two to the power of x equals six. So two to the power of x equals two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Same base.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x is one. The point is one, zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now stop, because the marking guideline for this question says two things out loud, and they are worth hearing.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The first. If a candidate interchanges question four point one point one and four point one point two, zero out of three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So if I give the x intercept where they asked for the y intercept.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You lose all three marks. Not one. All three. Even though both numbers on your page are correct.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is brutal.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It is, and it is printed. So read which intercept they asked for, and read it twice.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The second note. If a candidate says three, times two to the power of x, equals six to the power of x, award a maximum of one out of two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `But that would still give x equals one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It would. And you would still lose the mark, because the maths is wrong on the way. The three multiplies the power. It does not join the base.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the asymptote. Two to the power of x is always positive. It gets tiny, but it never reaches zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So three times it is also always positive.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And then minus six. So the whole thing is always a little more than negative six, and never equal to it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So the asymptote is the line y equals negative six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the range follows straight from it. y is greater than negative six.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Greater than. Not greater than or equal to.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Never equal to. It is an asymptote — the graph does not touch it. That equal sign is a mark, and it is the one learners hand back.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Second shape. The hyperbola. This one is November two thousand and twenty one, question five. Ten marks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Given, f of x equals negative one, divided by x minus three, and then plus two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The x is on the bottom, so it is a hyperbola.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And a hyperbola is really two asymptotes with a curve draped around them. Find those first, always.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The vertical one. What value of x would you never be allowed to use?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three. Because x minus three would be zero, and you cannot divide by zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the vertical asymptote is the line x equals three. And the horizontal one is whatever is added on the end.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Which is two. So y equals two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is why. As x gets enormous, negative one divided by a huge number shrinks towards nothing. All that is left is the two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So the curve flattens out towards y equals two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two marks, straight off the equation. Now the domain, for one mark.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x can be anything except three. So x is a real number, x not equal to three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the range is y not equal to two, for the same reason. Now the intercepts.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x intercept, so y is zero. Negative one, divided by x minus three, plus two, equals zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Move the two across, then clear the fraction.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative one divided by x minus three, equals negative two. So negative one equals negative two, times x minus three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Which gives two x minus six equals one. So two x is seven, and x is three comma five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The point is three comma five, zero. And the y intercept.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Make x zero. Negative one, divided by negative three, is one third. Plus two is two and a third.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Which the memo writes as two comma three three. So the point is zero, two comma three three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And then part five. Draw it, showing all asymptotes and intercepts. Three marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three marks for a drawing?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One for the shape, one for the asymptotes, one for the intercepts. Which means the dotted lines are not decoration. Leave them out and you have thrown a mark away.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Third shape. The parabola, and it comes with a straight line. November two thousand and twelve, question four point two. Thirteen marks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `S, at negative two, zero, and T, at six, zero, are the x intercepts of f of x equals a x squared plus b x plus c. And R is the y intercept.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The straight line through R and T is g of x equals negative two x plus d.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `There is no picture in front of me and I still know where everything is.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is the point. Read the words and the graph builds itself. Part one. Find d. Two marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The line goes through T, and T is six, zero. So I put those in.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Zero equals negative two times six, plus d. So zero equals negative twelve plus d, and d is twelve.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So g of x is negative two x plus twelve. And that hands you something else for free.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And while we have that line in front of us, run the whole characteristics list on it, because a straight line is a question all on its own.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `It is already in standard form, so the gradient is negative two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the intercepts.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The y intercept is twelve, so the point is zero, twelve. And the x intercept is T, at six, zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Domain and range.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `A straight line carries on forever in both directions. So x is any real number, and y is any real number.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And is it increasing or decreasing?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The gradient is negative, so it falls as you go right. Decreasing.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `That is the full list for a straight line, and when they ask for it, it is five quick marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `R. Because R is on the line too, and R is the y intercept, so x is zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `g of zero is twelve. So R is zero, twelve. And R is on the parabola as well.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three points on the parabola now. Part two, four marks. Find the equation of f.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And here is the shortcut. When you know both x intercepts, write the parabola in factor form.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `y equals a, bracket x minus six, bracket x plus two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Because those brackets are zero exactly at six and at negative two. Now use R to find a.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Twelve equals a, times negative six, times two. So twelve equals negative twelve a, and a is negative one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Multiply it out.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative one, times x squared minus four x minus twelve. Which is negative x squared, plus four x, plus twelve.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the guideline says something blunt about this part. No marks for answer only.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So even if I somehow guessed it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You would get nothing. Four marks, and every one of them is a line of working.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part three. The turning point. Two marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Can I use the two intercepts? The turning point should sit halfway between them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It does, and the memo accepts that. Negative two and six, halfway is two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Then y is negative four, plus eight, plus twelve. Which is sixteen. So the turning point is two, sixteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part four, and this is the one that separates people. For which values of k will f of x equal k have two distinct roots?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `I do not even know what that is asking.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It is asking where a flat horizontal line, at height k, cuts the parabola twice.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Oh. So I picture a line sliding up and down.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Slide it up. At height sixteen it just touches the top, once. Above sixteen it misses completely.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And below sixteen it cuts through both sides. Twice.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So k is less than sixteen. One mark, and no working needed — but only if you can see the picture.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Read which intercept they asked for. Swapping them scores zero, even with both numbers right.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. On a sketch, draw the asymptotes as dotted lines. They carry their own mark.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. A range that sits against an asymptote is greater than, never greater than or equal to.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. Where the x sits tells you the shape. On its own, squared, underneath, or up as the power.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. For a hyperbola and an exponential, find the asymptotes first. Everything else hangs off them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your question, and it is a real one. November two thousand and twelve, question six. Four marks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A hyperbola has domain, x is a real number, x not equal to five. Range, y is a real number, y not equal to one. And it passes through the point two, zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Determine f of x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The domain and the range are telling you the asymptotes. Start there.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Next time, what a function actually is, and why some graphs do not qualify. Episode two. Functions and relations.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:   0, title: `FUNCTIONS • EPISODE 1 • REVISION`, onScreen: `EPISODE 1 — REVISION: THE FOUR GRAPHS` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:   1, title: `FROM HERE ON, THE ANSWERS ARE PICTURES`, onScreen: `a new series, and a change of shape` },
    { n:  3, afterSeg:   3, title: `YOUR HOMEWORK`, onScreen: `x = (3 ± −k − 4)/(2) · for which values of k are the roots real?` },
    { n:  4, afterSeg:   4, title: `REAL MEANS THE DISCRIMINANT IS NOT NEGATIVE`, onScreen: `−k − 4 ≥ 0` },
    { n:  5, afterSeg:   5, title: `SO THE CONDITION IS`, onScreen: `−k ≥ 4` },
    { n:  6, afterSeg:   7, title: `AND NOW DIVIDE BY NEGATIVE ONE`, onScreen: `the sign turns around` },
    { n:  7, afterSeg:   8, title: `SO`, onScreen: `k ≤ −4 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n:  8, afterSeg:   9, title: `TEST IT WITH K = −5`, onScreen: `1 = 1 · real ✓ ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n:  9, afterSeg:  11, title: `FUNCTIONS`, onScreen: `FUNCTIONS` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg:  13, title: `GRADE 12 DOES NOT TEACH THESE AGAIN`, onScreen: `It assumes them, and builds inverses on top.` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg:  14, title: `IT ASSUMES THEM, AND BUILDS INVERSES ON TOP`, onScreen: `so this episode is the ground floor` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg:  15, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • WHERE THE GRAPHS LIVE IN PAPER 1`, onScreen: `QUESTIONS 4, 5 AND 6 · where the graphs live in Paper 1 · roughly 40 to 50 marks` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg:  17, title: `FOUR SHAPES`, onScreen: `the straight line · the parabola · the hyperbola · the exponential` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg:  19, title: `MARK BREAKDOWN`, onScreen: `on its own (a straight line) · squared (a parabola) · underneath (a hyperbola) · up as the power (an exponential)` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg:  20, title: `READ THE EQUATION FIRST`, onScreen: `The shape is decided before you plot a single point.` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg:  21, title: `WHERE THE X SITS TELLS YOU THE SHAPE`, onScreen: `before you draw anything at all` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg:  23, title: `AND THE EXAMINER WANTS THE SAME LIST`, onScreen: `intercepts · asymptotes · turning point · domain · range` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg:  24, title: `THOSE FIVE WORDS ARE THE MARKS`, onScreen: `not the drawing — the words` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg:  25, title: `WORTH DOING PROPERLY`, onScreen: `A word you only half know will stop you dead in the exam.` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg:  26, title: `AN INTERCEPT`, onScreen: `for the y-intercept make x = 0 · for the x-intercept make y = 0` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg:  28, title: `AN ASYMPTOTE`, onScreen: `A line the graph gets closer and closer to, but never touches.` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg:  30, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `DOMAIN the x values you are allowed to use — what goes in · RANGE the y values that come out — what comes out` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg:  32, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `INCREASING the y values climb as you move right · DECREASING the y values fall` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg:  34, title: `A PARABOLA DOES BOTH`, onScreen: `the turning point is where it changes over` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg:  35, title: `BUT FIRST — GET y ON ITS OWN`, onScreen: `BUT FIRST — GET y ON ITS OWN` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg:  37, title: `YOU CAN READ NOTHING OFF THIS`, onScreen: `red · no gradient, no intercept ✗` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg:  38, title: `SO DIVIDE THROUGH BY THREE`, onScreen: `y = 2x − 3` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg:  39, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `gradient 2 — sitting there · y-intercept −3 — sitting there` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg:  40, title: `THE FOUR STANDARD FORMS`, onScreen: `y = mx + c · y = a(x + p)² + q · y = (a)/(x + p) + q · y = abx + p + q` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg:  41, title: `STANDARD FORM FIRST, EVERY TIME`, onScreen: `it is the first line of working in nearly every one of these` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg:  42, title: `THREE REAL EXAM QUESTIONS NOW`, onScreen: `one shape at a time` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg:  43, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • NOVEMBER 2012`, onScreen: `NSC Mathematics P1 · November 2012 · Question 4.1 · 7 marks` },
