// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FUNCTIONS — EPISODE 2: Functions and Relations
// Paste into the EPISODES array in scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs,
// next to the existing series: 'Functions' existing series objects.
// Slides: unzip into podcasts/Working-It-Out/Functions/slides/ep02
// Run:    node --env-file=.env.local scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs Functions:2
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  num: 2,
  series: 'Functions',
  file: 'Episode 2 - Functions and Relations',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode two. Functions and relations.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Last time we put the four graphs back on the table. Today we ask what a graph actually is.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Homework first.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A hyperbola. Its domain is all real x, except x equals five. Its range is all real y, except y equals one. And it passes through the point two, zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Find the equation. Four marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `This one felt backwards. Usually they give me the equation and ask for the domain.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Which is exactly why they set it. Start with the shape of a hyperbola.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `a, divided by x minus p, plus q.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Good. Now, where does a hyperbola break?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Where the bottom is zero. And they told me it breaks at five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the bottom is x minus five. That is your first mark, and you have not calculated anything yet.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And the range. It never reaches one, so the whole graph is lifted by one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Second mark. So far, f of x is a, divided by x minus five, plus one. One unknown left.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And the point. Two, zero. So I put two in for x and zero in for y.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Zero equals a, divided by two minus five, plus one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Two minus five is negative three. So zero equals a, divided by negative three, plus one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `a divided by negative three is negative one. So a is three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the answer. f of x is three, divided by x minus five, plus one. Check it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three, divided by two minus five, is three divided by negative three, which is negative one. And negative one plus one is zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And be clear about this picture. The paper gives you no graph here. It asks for the equation, nothing else. I am drawing it only so you can watch the answer land on two, zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It goes through two, zero. Four marks, and three of them were handed to you by the domain and the range.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now. Today.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `What is the difference between a relation and a function? Because my textbook uses both words on the same page.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A relation is any rule that links things you put in to things that come out. That is all. It is a very loose word.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A function is a relation with one extra promise. Every input gives exactly one output.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Exactly one. Not two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Not two. Put a number in, and there must be no argument about what comes out.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Give me one that breaks the promise.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `y squared equals x. Put four in for x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `y squared is four. So y is two, or y is negative two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two answers from one input. So that is a relation, and it is not a function.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And y equals x squared?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Put two in, you get four. Put negative two in, you get four. Same answer twice.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `But each input still only gave one output.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Exactly. That is allowed. Two inputs may share an output. One input may not have two outputs.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So the promise is only about the way in.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Only about the way in. Say that to yourself in the exam.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And there is a picture of that promise. It is called the vertical line test.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Take any vertical line and slide it across the graph. If it ever touches the graph twice at once, it is not a function.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Because a vertical line is one x value.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One x value, and two points on it means two answers. The test is the promise, drawn.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So a circle fails.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `A circle fails. A parabola lying on its side fails. Your four graphs from last episode all pass.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Next. The notation. And this is where marks go missing quietly.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `f of x is not f times x. The f is a name, not a number.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So what is f of two?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It is an instruction. It says, take the rule called f, and put two in wherever you see an x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And whatever comes out is the answer.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Whatever comes out is the answer. Now here is the sentence I want you to remember for the rest of the year.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `If a point lies on the graph, then its two numbers make the equation true.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Say that again.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `If the point three, negative nine lies on the graph of f, then f of three equals negative nine. Substitute, and it works.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So lies on means substitute.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Lies on means substitute. That one translation turns a picture into an equation you can solve, and it is worth marks in almost every functions question they set.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now, domain and range.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The domain is every x you are allowed to put in. The range is every y that comes out.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `How do I know what I am allowed to put in?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You are allowed everything, until the equation stops you. And in Grade twelve there are only two things that stop you.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. You may not divide by zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. You may not take the square root of a negative.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is the whole list?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `For this paper, that is the whole list. So look at your equation and ask, is there a bottom, and is there a root. If there is neither, the domain is all real numbers.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So a straight line and a parabola take anything.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Anything at all. A hyperbola has a bottom, so one x is missing. A square root graph has a root, so half the number line is missing.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And the range?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The range you read off the shape. A parabola turns, so it stops at its turning point. An exponential never reaches its asymptote.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `But hear this clearly. The restriction is a fact about the equation. The picture only shows you what the equation already decided.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example one, and it is built out of that exact idea.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, November two thousand and nineteen. Question four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You are given f of x equals x squared, plus b x, minus three. And g of x equals a, divided by x plus p.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `f cuts the x axis at the point one, zero. D is the y intercept of both f and g. And the vertical asymptote of g passes through the x intercept of f.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And all of that comes with a sketch. The paper prints both graphs above the question, with C, D, E and J marked on them. You are reading a picture, not building one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Question four has seven parts and nineteen marks. We take three of them today, the three about domain, range, and reading a sentence.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part one. Write down the value of p. One mark.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `There are three unknowns and no numbers to work with.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `There are numbers. They are just written as sentences. Take the last sentence first. Where is the vertical asymptote of g?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Where the bottom is zero. So x plus p equals zero, which means x is negative p.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the sentence says that line goes through the x intercept of f.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Which they told me is one. So negative p is one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So p is negative one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One mark, one sentence, no working. Part two. Show that a equals three, and b equals two. Three marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Show that. So they have given me the answers.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `They have given you the answers, and they are paying you for the road. Which fact have you not used yet?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That f goes through one, zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Lies on means substitute.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So f of one equals zero. One squared, plus b times one, minus three, equals zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `One plus b minus three is zero. So b minus two is zero, and b is two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `There is b. Now a. Where does D come from?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `D is the y intercept. So I put zero in.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `f of zero is zero, plus zero, minus three. So D is the point zero, negative three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And read the sentence again. D is the y intercept of both graphs.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So D is on g as well. g of zero is a, divided by zero plus negative one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Which is a, divided by negative one. So that is negative a.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And it has to equal the y value of D.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative a equals negative three. So a is three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three marks. The coordinates of D, the substitution into f, and the substitution into g. Every one of them is a substitution.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And the word both was doing all the work.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The word both was the whole question. Read those sentences twice.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part three asks for the coordinates of C, the turning point. Four marks. We are not taking it on its own today, but part four makes you find that same point anyway.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Same question, part four. Write down the range of f. Two marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `f of x is x squared, plus two x, minus three, now that I know b.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And it is a parabola that smiles, so it has a lowest point and nothing below it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So I need the turning point.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Complete the square, or use the axis of symmetry. Your choice, the memo pays for both.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative b divided by two a. Negative two, divided by two, is negative one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And f of negative one is one, minus two, minus three. Which is negative four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the lowest y value is negative four, and it is reached.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So y is greater than or equal to negative four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Both marks are on that line. And notice the equal to. The parabola actually touches negative four, so it is included.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is where we leave question four. Parts five, six and seven carry nine more marks, on lines, tangents and shifts. Different topics, and they get their own episodes.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One more idea, and it is the door into next episode.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Take that parabola and pick a y value above the turning point. Say y equals zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x squared plus two x minus three is zero. That factorises to x plus three, times x minus one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x is negative three, or x is one. Two of them.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two inputs, one output. We call that many to one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And the straight line?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Every y comes from exactly one x. That is one to one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Does it matter which one it is?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `It matters enormously, and next episode is about why. Hold the words.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example three. Reading a restriction off a graph, and one mark that almost everybody loses.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, November two thousand and twelve. Question five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The graph of f of x equals negative the square root of twenty seven x, for x greater than or equal to zero, is sketched. The point P, three, negative nine, lies on it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Use your graph to determine the values of x for which f of x is greater than or equal to negative nine. Two marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `First, is P really on it? f of three is negative the square root of twenty seven times three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Twenty seven times three is eighty one. The square root of eighty one is nine. So f of three is negative nine.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Good. Never trust a labelled point until you have checked it. Now the question. And remember, this graph is printed in the paper for you. You read it. You do not draw it.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Greater than or equal to negative nine means at or above the height of P.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And this graph falls as you go right. So where does it stop being high enough?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `At P itself. So x can go up to three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And where does it start?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `At zero, because the domain will not let me go left of that. There is a square root.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the answer is, x is greater than or equal to zero, and less than or equal to three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Zero to three, with both ends included.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now listen to what the marking guideline actually prints next to this question.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `If the candidate gives zero less than x, less than three, award one out of two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So writing it without the equal to costs a mark.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One mark for each end. Drop the equal signs and you keep half the marks. That is the cheapest mark in the paper to lose.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Parts two and three of this question ask you for the inverse, and then for its sketch. Six marks between them. That is next episode, so we go straight to the last part.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Last part. If g of x is the square root of twenty seven x, where x is greater than or equal to zero, describe the transformation from f to g. One mark.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `That is the same thing without the minus.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So g is negative f. Every y value has had its sign flipped.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `A reflection about the x axis.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One mark. And the memo takes those exact words, or the mapping x, y to x, minus y. Write one of those two. The second curve here is mine — the paper never asks you to draw g.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Lies on means substitute. If a question hands you a point, it is handing you an equation.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. Restrictions come from the equation, not from the drawing. A bottom, or a root. Those are the only two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. Include the equal to. A closed end is a mark of its own, every time.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. A function promises one output for every input. Nothing about the way out.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. The domain is what the equation allows. The range is what the shape delivers.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your homework. The graph of f of x equals k to the power x, where k is greater than zero, is sketched, and the point four, sixteen lies on it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Determine the value of k. Two marks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Lies on means substitute. I know where to start.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Next time. What happens when you run a function backwards.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Inverse functions.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And why one to one suddenly matters.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:   0, title: `FUNCTIONS • EPISODE 2 • FUNCTIONS AND RELATIONS`, onScreen: `EPISODE 2 — FUNCTIONS AND RELATIONS` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:   1, title: `TODAY WE ASK WHAT A GRAPH IS`, onScreen: `last episode we drew them — now we define them` },
    { n:  3, afterSeg:   2, title: `HOMEWORK FIRST`, onScreen: `HOMEWORK FIRST` },
    { n:  4, afterSeg:   3, title: `MARK BREAKDOWN`, onScreen: `Domain: x ∈ R, x ≠ 5 () · Range: y ∈ R, y ≠ 1 () · Passes through the point (2 ; 0) () · Determine f(x). ([4])` },
    { n:  5, afterSeg:   4, title: `Q6`, onScreen: `Determine f(x). · no equation given — only the restrictions and one point` },
    { n:  6, afterSeg:   5, title: `THEY GAVE YOU THE RESTRICTIONS AND ASKED FOR…`, onScreen: `the usual question, run backwards` },
    { n:  7, afterSeg:   7, title: `THE SHAPE OF EVERY HYPERBOLA`, onScreen: `y = (a)/(x − p) + q` },
    { n:  8, afterSeg:   9, title: `A HYPERBOLA BREAKS WHERE THE BOTTOM IS ZERO`, onScreen: `x − p = 0 → x = p → and they said x ≠ 5` },
    { n:  9, afterSeg:  10, title: `MARK ONE — AND NOTHING WAS CALCULATED`, onScreen: `f(x) = (a)/(x − 5) + q ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg:  11, title: `THE RANGE LIFTS THE WHOLE GRAPH`, onScreen: `y ≠ 1 → q = 1` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg:  12, title: `MARK TWO — ONE UNKNOWN LEFT`, onScreen: `f(x) = (a)/(x − 5) + 1 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg:  13, title: `LIES ON MEANS SUBSTITUTE`, onScreen: `put 2 in for x and 0 in for y` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg:  14, title: `SUBSTITUTE THE POINT`, onScreen: `0 = (a)/(2 − 5) + 1` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg:  15, title: `SIMPLIFY THE BOTTOM`, onScreen: `0 = (a)/(−3) + 1` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg:  16, title: `MARK THREE AND FOUR`, onScreen: `a = 3 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg:  17, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `f(x) = (3)/(x − 5) + 1 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg:  18, title: `AND IT DOES GO THROUGH (2 ; 0)`, onScreen: `f(x) = (3)/(x − 5) + 1 · asymptotes x = 5 · y = 1` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg:  19, title: `THE PAPER GIVES YOU NO PICTURE HERE`, onScreen: `You are not asked to draw it, and there are no marks for drawing it.` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg:  20, title: `POINT 3 of 4 • MARKS CAME FROM THE RESTRICTIONS`, onScreen: `marks came from the restrictions — before a single calculation` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg:  21, title: `FUNCTIONS AND RELATIONS`, onScreen: `FUNCTIONS AND RELATIONS · the promise a graph makes` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg:  23, title: `RELATION`, onScreen: `a very loose word — almost everything qualifies` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg:  24, title: `FUNCTION`, onScreen: `the extra promise` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg:  26, title: `PUT A NUMBER IN — THERE MUST BE NO ARGUMENT…`, onScreen: `put a number in — there must be no argument about what comes out` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg:  28, title: `BREAK THE PROMISE`, onScreen: `y² = x · put x = 4 in` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg:  29, title: `ONE INPUT, TWO OUTPUTS`, onScreen: `red · y² = 4 → y = 2 or y = −2` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg:  30, title: `SO IT IS A RELATION, NOT A FUNCTION`, onScreen: `red · y² = x ✗ — the promise is broken at every positive x` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg:  31, title: `NOW THE OTHER WAY ROUND`, onScreen: `y = x² · is this one a function?` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg:  32, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `x = 2 y = 4 · x = −2 y = 4` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg:  34, title: `THE RULE, EXACTLY`, onScreen: `two inputs may share an output ✓ · one input may not have two outputs ✗` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg:  35, title: `THE PROMISE IS ONLY ABOUT THE WAY IN`, onScreen: `say it to yourself in the exam` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg:  37, title: `THE VERTICAL LINE TEST`, onScreen: `THE VERTICAL LINE TEST` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg:  38, title: `VERTICAL LINE TEST`, onScreen: `the promise, drawn` },
    { n: 33, afterSeg:  39, title: `ONE VERTICAL LINE IS ONE X VALUE`, onScreen: `y² = x · asymptotes x = 4` },
    { n: 34, afterSeg:  42, title: `WHO FAILS THE TEST`, onScreen: `a circle ✗ · a parabola lying on its side ✗ · all four of last episode’s graphs ✓` },
    { n: 35, afterSeg:  43, title: `FUNCTION NOTATION`, onScreen: `FUNCTION NOTATION` },
    { n: 36, afterSeg:  44, title: `F(X) IS NOT A MULTIPLICATION`, onScreen: `red · f(x) ≠ f × x ✗ — f is a name, not a number` },
    { n: 37, afterSeg:  46, title: `f(2)`, onScreen: `it is an instruction, not a product` },
    { n: 38, afterSeg:  48, title: `THE SENTENCE OF THE YEAR`, onScreen: `THE SENTENCE OF THE YEAR` },
    { n: 39, afterSeg:  49, title: `REMEMBER THIS ONE`, onScreen: `if a point lies on the graph,its two numbers make the equation true` },
    { n: 40, afterSeg:  51, title: `AN EXAMPLE OF THE TRANSLATION`, onScreen: `P(3 ; −9) lies on f → f(3) = −9 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 41, afterSeg:  53, title: `LIES ON = SUBSTITUTE`, onScreen: `it turns a picture into an equation you can solve` },
    { n: 42, afterSeg:  54, title: `DOMAIN AND RANGE`, onScreen: `DOMAIN AND RANGE` },
    { n: 43, afterSeg:  55, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `DOMAIN every x you are allowed to put in · RANGE every y that comes out` },
    { n: 44, afterSeg:  57, title: `YOU ARE ALLOWED EVERYTHING — UNTIL THE…`, onScreen: `you are allowed everything — until the equation stops you` },
    { n: 45, afterSeg:  58, title: `POINT 1 • YOU MAY NOT DIVIDE BY ZERO`, onScreen: `you may not divide by zero — look for a bottom` },
    { n: 46, afterSeg:  59, title: `POINT 2 • YOU MAY NOT SQUARE-ROOT A NEGATIVE`, onScreen: `you may not square-root a negative — look for a root` },
    { n: 47, afterSeg:  61, title: `SO THE WHOLE CHECK IS TWO QUESTIONS`, onScreen: `is there a bottom? · is there a root? · neither → the domain is all of ℝ` },
    { n: 48, afterSeg:  62, title: `MARK BREAKDOWN`, onScreen: `no bottom, no root (x ∈ ℝ) · no bottom, no root (x ∈ ℝ)` },
    { n: 49, afterSeg:  63, title: `MARK BREAKDOWN`, onScreen: `a bottom → one x missing (x ≠ p) · a root → half the line missing (x ≥ 0)` },
    { n: 50, afterSeg:  65, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `DOMAIN what the equation allows · RANGE what the shape delivers` },
    { n: 51, afterSeg:  66, title: `THE POINT OF THE WHOLE EPISODE`, onScreen: `the restriction is a fact about the equationthe picture only shows what the equation already decided` },
    { n: 52, afterSeg:  67, title: `EXAMPLE ONE`, onScreen: `EXAMPLE ONE` },
    { n: 53, afterSeg:  68, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • MATHEMATICS PAPER 1`, onScreen: `NSC · NOVEMBER 2019 · Mathematics Paper 1 · Question 4` },
    { n: 54, afterSeg:  69, title: `NSC NOV 2019 · P1 · QUESTION 4 · [19]`, onScreen: `Below are the graphs off(x) = x² + bx − 3 and g(x) = (a)/(x + p) . · the paper prints both graphs` },
    { n: 55, afterSeg:  70, title: `PRINTED WITH THE QUESTION`, onScreen: `f has a turning point at C and passes through the x-axis at (1 ; 0). · D is the y-intercept of both f and g. The graphs f and g also intersect each other at E and J. · The vertical asymptote of g passes through the x-intercept of f.` },
    { n: 56, afterSeg:  71, title: `THE SKETCH IS PRINTED WITH THE QUESTION`, onScreen: `You are reading a picture here, not building one.` },
    { n: 57, afterSeg:  72, title: `MARK BREAKDOWN`, onScreen: `Write down the value of p. ((1)) · Show that a = 3 and b = 2. ((3)) · Write down the range of f. ((2))` },
    { n: 58, afterSeg:  73, title: `Q4.1`, onScreen: `Write down the value of p. · one mark, and no calculation in it` },
    { n: 59, afterSeg:  75, title: `THE NUMBERS ARE THERE — THEY ARE WRITTEN AS…`, onScreen: `the numbers are there — they are written as sentences` },
    { n: 60, afterSeg:  76, title: `WHERE IS THE ASYMPTOTE OF G?`, onScreen: `x + p = 0 → x = −p` },
    { n: 61, afterSeg:  77, title: `AND THAT LINE GOES THROUGH THE X-INTERCEPT OF F`, onScreen: `−p = 1` },
    { n: 62, afterSeg:  79, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `p = −1 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 63, afterSeg:  80, title: `Q4.2`, onScreen: `Show that a = 3 and b = 2. · they have given you the answers — they are paying for the road` },
    { n: 64, afterSeg:  82, title: `WHICH FACT HAVE YOU NOT USED YET?`, onScreen: `which fact have you not used yet?` },
    { n: 65, afterSeg:  84, title: `THE ONLY TOOL YOU NEED`, onScreen: `lies on → substitute` },
    { n: 66, afterSeg:  85, title: `F GOES THROUGH (1 ; 0)`, onScreen: `1² + b(1) − 3 = 0` },
    { n: 67, afterSeg:  86, title: `MARK — B IS OUT`, onScreen: `b = 2 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 68, afterSeg:  88, title: `D IS THE Y-INTERCEPT — SO PUT ZERO IN`, onScreen: `f(0) = 0 + 0 − 3` },
    { n: 69, afterSeg:  89, title: `MARK — THE COORDINATES OF D`, onScreen: `D(0 ; −3) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 70, afterSeg:  90, title: `D IS THE Y-INTERCEPT OF BOTH GRAPHS`, onScreen: `one word, and it is the whole question` },
    { n: 71, afterSeg:  91, title: `SO PUT ZERO INTO G AS WELL`, onScreen: `g(0) = (a)/(0 + (−1))` },
    { n: 72, afterSeg:  92, title: `SIMPLIFY`, onScreen: `g(0) = −a` },
    { n: 73, afterSeg:  94, title: `MARK — SET IT EQUAL TO D`, onScreen: `a = 3 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 74, afterSeg:  95, title: `MARK BREAKDOWN`, onScreen: `the coordinates of D (substitute) · into f (substitute) · into g (substitute)` },
    { n: 75, afterSeg:  97, title: `THE SKETCH THE PAPER GIVES YOU`, onScreen: `f(x) = x² + 2x − 3 · g(x) = (3)/(x − 1) · asymptotes x = 1` },
    { n: 76, afterSeg:  98, title: `PART THREE — NOT TAKEN TODAY`, onScreen: `Keep watching — the range in 4.4 makes you find that same turning point anyway.` },
    { n: 77, afterSeg:  99, title: `Q4.4`, onScreen: `Write down the range of f. · now that b is known, f is fully determined` },
    { n: 78, afterSeg: 100, title: `NSC NOV 2019 · P1 · Q4.4`, onScreen: `f(x) = x² + 2x − 3 · the range` },
    { n: 79, afterSeg: 101, title: `IT SMILES — SO THERE IS A LOWEST POINT AND…`, onScreen: `it smiles — so there is a lowest point and nothing below it` },
    { n: 80, afterSeg: 103, title: `COMPLETE THE SQUARE VS AXIS OF SYMMETRY`, onScreen: `the memo pays for both` },
    { n: 81, afterSeg: 104, title: `THE TURNING POINT`, onScreen: `x = (−2)/(2) = −1` },
    { n: 82, afterSeg: 105, title: `SUBSTITUTE IT BACK`, onScreen: `f(−1) = 1 − 2 − 3 = −4` },
    { n: 83, afterSeg: 107, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `y ≥ −4 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 84, afterSeg: 108, title: `AND IT REACHES IT — SO THE EQUAL TO STAYS`, onScreen: `f(x) = x² + 2x − 3` },
    { n: 85, afterSeg: 109, title: `AND THAT IS WHERE WE LEAVE QUESTION 4`, onScreen: `Different topics. They get their own episodes.` },
    { n: 86, afterSeg: 110, title: `ONE MORE IDEA`, onScreen: `ONE MORE IDEA` },
    { n: 87, afterSeg: 111, title: `PICK A y ABOVE THE TURNING POINT`, onScreen: `y = 0 · how many x values give it?` },
    { n: 88, afterSeg: 112, title: `FACTORISE`, onScreen: `x² + 2x − 3 = 0 → (x + 3)(x − 1) = 0` },
    { n: 89, afterSeg: 113, title: `TWO INPUTS, ONE OUTPUT`, onScreen: `x = −3 or x = 1` },
    { n: 90, afterSeg: 114, title: `MANY-TO-ONE`, onScreen: `every parabola is many-to-one` },
    { n: 91, afterSeg: 116, title: `ONE-TO-ONE`, onScreen: `straight line · exponential · hyperbola` },
    { n: 92, afterSeg: 118, title: `NEXT • INVERSE FUNCTIONS`, onScreen: `NEXT — EPISODE 3: INVERSE FUNCTIONS` },
    { n: 93, afterSeg: 119, title: `EXAMPLE THREE`, onScreen: `EXAMPLE THREE` },
    { n: 94, afterSeg: 120, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • MATHEMATICS PAPER 1`, onScreen: `NSC · NOVEMBER 2012 · Mathematics Paper 1 · Question 5` },
    { n: 95, afterSeg: 121, title: `NSC NOV 2012 · P1 · QUESTION 5 · [9]`, onScreen: `The graph of f(x) = −27x for x ≥ 0 is sketched below. · The point P(3 ; −9) lies on the graph of f.` },
    { n: 96, afterSeg: 122, title: `Q5.1`, onScreen: `Use your graph to determine the values of x for which f(x) ≥ −9. · two marks — and there is one in each end` },
    { n: 97, afterSeg: 123, title: `FIRST — CHECK THE LABELLED POINT`, onScreen: `f(3) = −27 × 3` },
    { n: 98, afterSeg: 124, title: `IT IS REALLY ON THE GRAPH`, onScreen: `f(3) = −9 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 99, afterSeg: 125, title: `NEVER TRUST A LABELLED POINT UNTIL YOU HAVE…`, onScreen: `never trust a labelled point until you have checked it` },
    { n: 100, afterSeg: 126, title: `AT OR ABOVE THE HEIGHT OF P`, onScreen: `f(x) = −27x` },
    { n: 101, afterSeg: 128, title: `THE RIGHT-HAND END`, onScreen: `x ≤ 3` },
    { n: 102, afterSeg: 130, title: `AND THE LEFT-HAND END COMES FROM THE DOMAIN`, onScreen: `x ≥ 0 (there is a root)` },
    { n: 103, afterSeg: 131, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `0 ≤ x ≤ 3 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 104, afterSeg: 133, title: `WHAT THE MEMO ACTUALLY PRINTS`, onScreen: `WHAT THE MEMO ACTUALLY PRINTS` },
    { n: 105, afterSeg: 134, title: `THE MARKING GUIDELINE, WORD FOR WORD`, onScreen: `red · 0 < x < 3 ✗ — “if the candidate gives 0 < x < 3, award 1⁄2 marks”` },
    { n: 106, afterSeg: 136, title: `POINT 1 • ONE MARK FOR EACH END`, onScreen: `one mark for each end — drop the equal signs and you keep half` },
    { n: 107, afterSeg: 137, title: `PARTS TWO AND THREE — NEXT EPISODE`, onScreen: `Both are inverse functions, which is where episode 3 starts.` },
    { n: 108, afterSeg: 138, title: `Q5.4`, onScreen: `Describe the transformation from f to g if g(x) = 27x, where x ≥ 0. · one mark, one sentence` },
    { n: 109, afterSeg: 139, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `f(x) = −27x the one you were given · g(x) = 27x the same rule, without the minus` },
    { n: 110, afterSeg: 140, title: `IT IS THE SAME RULE WITHOUT THE MINUS`, onScreen: `g(x) = −f(x) → every y has flipped its sign` },
    { n: 111, afterSeg: 141, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `a reflection about the x-axis ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 112, afterSeg: 142, title: `THE SAME CURVE, FLIPPED`, onScreen: `f(x) = −27x · g(x) = 27x` },
    { n: 113, afterSeg: 143, title: `WHERE THE MARKS GO`, onScreen: `WHERE THE MARKS GO` },
    { n: 114, afterSeg: 144, title: `POINT 1 • LIES ON MEANS SUBSTITUTE`, onScreen: `lies on means substitute — a point is an equation in disguise` },
    { n: 115, afterSeg: 145, title: `POINT 2 • RESTRICTIONS COME FROM THE EQUATION`, onScreen: `restrictions come from the equation — a bottom, or a root — those are the only two` },
    { n: 116, afterSeg: 146, title: `POINT 3 • INCLUDE THE EQUAL TO`, onScreen: `include the equal to — a closed end is a mark of its own` },
    { n: 117, afterSeg: 147, title: `TWO THINGS TO REMEMBER`, onScreen: `TWO THINGS TO REMEMBER` },
    { n: 118, afterSeg: 148, title: `ONE`, onScreen: `a function promises one output for every inputnothing at all about the way out` },
    { n: 119, afterSeg: 149, title: `TWO`, onScreen: `the domain is what the equation allowsthe range is what the shape delivers` },
    { n: 120, afterSeg: 150, title: `YOUR HOMEWORK · NSC NOV 2019 · P1 · QUESTION 5`, onScreen: `Sketched below is the graph off(x) = kx ; k > 0. · The point (4 ; 16) lies on f.` },
    { n: 121, afterSeg: 151, title: `Q5.1`, onScreen: `Determine the value of k. · one point, one unknown — and you know the translation` },
    { n: 122, afterSeg: 152, title: `PAUSE AND TRY`, onScreen: `PAUSE AND TRY` },
    { n: 123, afterSeg: 153, title: `LIES ON MEANS SUBSTITUTE`, onScreen: `you already know where to start` },
    { n: 124, afterSeg: 154, title: `NEXT • INVERSE FUNCTIONS`, onScreen: `NEXT — EPISODE 3: INVERSE FUNCTIONS` },
    { n: 125, afterSeg: 156, title: `AND WHY ONE-TO-ONE MATTERS`, onScreen: `AND WHY ONE-TO-ONE MATTERS · Working It Out · Functions · Episode 3` },
  ],
},
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FUNCTIONS — EPISODE 3: Inverse Functions
// Paste into the EPISODES array in scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs,
// next to the existing series: 'Functions' existing series objects.
// Slides: unzip into podcasts/Working-It-Out/Functions/slides/ep03
// Run:    node --env-file=.env.local scripts/generate-maths-podcast-audio.mjs Functions:3
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  num: 3,
  series: 'Functions',
  file: 'Episode 3 - Inverse Functions',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `Episode three. Inverse functions.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Last time we said a function makes one promise. One input, one output. Today we run that promise backwards.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Homework first.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The graph of f of x equals k to the power x, where k is greater than zero, is sketched, and the point four, sixteen lies on it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Determine the value of k. Two marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Lies on means substitute. So f of four equals sixteen.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `k to the power four equals sixteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And sixteen is two to the power four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So k is two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two marks. One for the substitution, one for the answer. And notice they told you k is greater than zero. That is why you do not also write negative two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now. Today.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `What actually is an inverse?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is the whole thing in one sentence. An inverse swaps the two columns.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Which two columns?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Whatever went in, and whatever came out. If the function sends two to five, then the inverse sends five back to two.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So the input becomes the output.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The input becomes the output. Everything else you will ever learn about inverses is a consequence of that one sentence.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And on a graph, swapping the two numbers of every point is a reflection in the line y equals x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Let us watch that happen on a real exam question.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, November two thousand and twenty four. Question four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Given, f of x equals a to the power x, minus one, for a greater than zero. And the point B, two, negative five divided by nine, lies on f.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part one. Calculate the value of a. Two marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Lies on means substitute. Negative five divided by nine equals a squared, minus one.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So a squared is four divided by nine.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And a is two divided by three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `They said a is greater than zero, so two thirds it is. Now jump to part four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `C is a point on f at y equals nineteen divided by eight. Determine the coordinates of C dash, the image of C when C is reflected about the line y equals x. Three marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `First I need C itself. Two thirds to the power x, minus one, equals nineteen divided by eight.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So two thirds to the power x is twenty seven divided by eight.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And twenty seven divided by eight is three halves, cubed.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Three halves is two thirds, flipped. So that is two thirds to the power negative three.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So x is negative three. C is the point negative three, nineteen divided by eight.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And now the reflection. Swap the two numbers.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `C dash is nineteen divided by eight, negative three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three marks, and there was no new formula in it. You swapped a pair of numbers.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now the algebra. If swapping the numbers is the idea, then swapping the letters is the method.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `To find an inverse. Swap x and y, then solve for y again.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Swap, then solve.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Swap, then solve. Say it every single time, because the order is where people lose it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example two. The easiest shape there is.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, February and March two thousand and eighteen. Question four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The paper draws a parabola, f of x equals x minus four, all squared, minus nine, and a straight line g.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `C is the y intercept of both of them. And D is where g cuts the x axis.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Earlier parts of the question hand you those two points. C is zero, seven. D is four, zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part five. Write down the equation of g inverse in the form y equals. Three marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `I need g first. The gradient is the change in y, divided by the change in x.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Zero minus seven, divided by four minus zero. That is negative seven divided by four.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And it cuts the y axis at seven. So g of x is negative seven divided by four, x, plus seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now swap, then solve.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x equals negative seven divided by four, y, plus seven.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Multiply everything by four. Four x equals negative seven y, plus twenty eight.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So seven y equals negative four x, plus twenty eight.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And y is negative four divided by seven, x, plus four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three marks. And here is a check that costs you nothing.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The inverse of a straight line is a straight line, and the two intercepts swap.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `g went through zero, seven and four, zero. So g inverse goes through zero, four and seven, zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Which is exactly what that equation says. The marking guideline even prints that as an accepted alternative.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example three. This is the one that separates the marks.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, November two thousand and eighteen. Question four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `In the diagram, the graph of f of x equals a x squared is drawn, in the interval x less than or equal to zero. The graph of f inverse is also drawn.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `So the picture is given to you, and both curves are already on it. You are reading it, not building it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `P, negative six, negative twelve, is a point on f. And R is a point on f inverse.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part one. Is f inverse a function? Motivate your answer. Two marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `A parabola normally fails. Two inputs share an output.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Normally. Read the interval again.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x less than or equal to zero. They have only given me half the parabola.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And half a parabola is one to one. Every x has its own y, and no y is shared with anything.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So yes. f inverse is a function.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Yes. And the guideline wants a reason as well as the word yes. One to one mapping. Or, for every x value there is only one corresponding y value.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One mark for the yes, one mark for the reason. Never write only the word yes.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part two. If R is the reflection of P in the line y equals x, write down the coordinates of R. One mark.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Swap the numbers. R is negative twelve, negative six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One mark, no working, and it is the same swap you did ten minutes ago.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part three. Calculate the value of a. Two marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `P lies on f. So negative twelve equals a, times negative six, squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Negative six squared is thirty six. So a is negative one third.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part four. Write down the equation of f inverse in the form y equals. Three marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `f is y equals negative one third, x squared. So I swap.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x equals negative one third, y squared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So y squared is negative three x. And y is plus or minus the square root of negative three x.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Stop right there. Which sign?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `The picture. f inverse sits below the x axis, so y is negative.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And that is the restriction doing its work. The guideline prints, only y equals negative the square root of negative three x, and x less than or equal to zero.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Both the minus and the restriction.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Both. Leave either one off and you are handing marks back.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Example four. Same method, one shape further.` },
    { s: 'AMELIA',   t: `National Senior Certificate, Mathematics Paper One, November two thousand and twelve. Question five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `The graph of f of x equals negative the square root of twenty seven x, for x greater than or equal to zero, is sketched. The point P, three, negative nine, lies on it.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Part two. Write down the equation of f inverse in the form y equals. Include all restrictions. Three marks.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Swap. x equals negative the square root of twenty seven y.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Now square both sides. And watch what happens to that minus.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x squared equals twenty seven y. The minus disappeared.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `So y is x squared, divided by twenty seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `And the restriction?` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `f only ever gave out negative numbers. So the inverse can only take negative numbers in.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `x less than or equal to zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three marks. One for the swap, one for the equation, one for the restriction. The restriction is a third of the question.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `And squaring is what hid the minus.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Squaring is what hid the minus. That is exactly why they write, include all restrictions.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Three things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number one. Swap, then solve. Not solve, then swap.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number two. Motivate means write a reason. Yes on its own is one mark out of two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Number three. Include all restrictions. Wherever the domain was cut, the inverse remembers.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `One. An inverse swaps the two columns. Every other rule follows from that.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Two. A many to one function has no inverse function, until you cut its domain in half.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Here is your homework. Same paper, same question, part three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Sketch f inverse, the inverse of f, in your answer book. Indicate the intercepts with the axes and the coordinates of one other point. Three marks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `It is y equals x squared divided by twenty seven, with x less than or equal to zero. I already have the equation.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `You already have the equation. And if you think about P, you already have your other point too.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Next time. The exponential function, and the name we give to its inverse.` },
    { s: 'TYLA',     t: `Logarithms.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL',  t: `Logarithms. And you have already met one today, without being told.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n:  1, afterSeg:   0, title: `FUNCTIONS • EPISODE 3 • INVERSE FUNCTIONS`, onScreen: `EPISODE 3 — INVERSE FUNCTIONS` },
    { n:  2, afterSeg:   1, title: `TODAY WE RUN THE PROMISE BACKWARDS`, onScreen: `one input, one output — read the other way` },
    { n:  3, afterSeg:   2, title: `HOMEWORK FIRST`, onScreen: `HOMEWORK FIRST` },
    { n:  4, afterSeg:   3, title: `YOUR HOMEWORK · NSC NOV 2019 · P1 · QUESTION 5`, onScreen: `Sketched below is the graph off(x) = kx ; k > 0. · The point (4 ; 16) lies on f.` },
    { n:  5, afterSeg:   4, title: `QUESTION 5.1`, onScreen: `Determine the value of k. · one point, one unknown` },
    { n:  6, afterSeg:   5, title: `LIES ON MEANS SUBSTITUTE`, onScreen: `put 4 in for x and 16 in for y` },
    { n:  7, afterSeg:   6, title: `SUBSTITUTE THE POINT`, onScreen: `k⁴ = 16` },
    { n:  8, afterSeg:   7, title: `MAKE THE BASES MATCH`, onScreen: `16 = 2⁴` },
    { n:  9, afterSeg:   8, title: `ANSWER`, onScreen: `k = 2 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg:   9, title: `WHY NOT NEGATIVE TWO`, onScreen: `The restriction was there to stop you. Read it before you answer.` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg:  10, title: `INVERSE FUNCTIONS`, onScreen: `INVERSE FUNCTIONS · running the rule backwards` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg:  12, title: `AN INVERSE SWAPS THE TWO COLUMNS`, onScreen: `every other rule about inverses follows from this one sentence` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg:  14, title: `IN, THEN OUT`, onScreen: `2 → f → 5` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg:  15, title: `COMPARE`, onScreen: `f : 2 → 5 the function · f&miⁿus;¹ : 5 → 2 the inverse` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg:  16, title: `THE INPUT BECOMES THE OUTPUT`, onScreen: `that is the whole definition` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg:  17, title: `AND ON A GRAPH`, onScreen: `swapping the two numbers of every pointis a reflection in the line y = x` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg:  18, title: `EXAMPLE ONE`, onScreen: `EXAMPLE ONE` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg:  19, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • MATHEMATICS PAPER 1`, onScreen: `NSC · NOVEMBER 2024 · Mathematics Paper 1 · Question 4` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg:  20, title: `NSC NOV 2024 · P1 · QUESTION 4 · [9]`, onScreen: `Given: f(x) = ax − 1 for a > 0. · B(2 ; (−5)/(9) ) is a point on f.` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg:  21, title: `QUESTION 4.1`, onScreen: `Calculate the value of a. · the point is the equation` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg:  22, title: `LIES ON MEANS SUBSTITUTE`, onScreen: `(−5)/(9) = a² − 1` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg:  23, title: `ADD ONE TO BOTH SIDES`, onScreen: `a² = (4)/(9)` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg:  24, title: `AND A IS POSITIVE`, onScreen: `a = (2)/(3) ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg:  26, title: `QUESTION 4.4`, onScreen: `It is further given that C is a point on f at y = (19)/(8) . Determine the coordinates of C′, the image of C, when C is reflected about the line y = x. · find C first — then swap` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg:  27, title: `FIND C — PUT THE Y IN`, onScreen: `( (2)/(3) )x − 1 = (19)/(8)` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg:  28, title: `ADD ONE`, onScreen: `( (2)/(3) )x = (27)/(8)` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg:  29, title: `WRITE THE RIGHT SIDE AS A CUBE`, onScreen: `(27)/(8) = ( (3)/(2) )³` },
